window.CXSearchIndex = [{"slug":"bindings/c-abi","title":"C ABI","summary":"Every libcx symbol that bindings link against. UTF-8 in, UTF-8 out, capability bits gate features.","text":"C ABI C ABI Every libcx symbol that bindings link against. UTF-8 in, UTF-8 out, capability bits gate features. Format Conversion c char *cx_to_cx (const char *in, char **err); char *cx_to_xml (const char *in, char **err); char *cx_to_json (const char *in, char **err); char *cx_to_yaml (const char *in, char **err); char *cx_to_toml (const char *in, char **err); char *cx_to_md (const char *in, char **err); char *cx_to_csv (const char *in, char **err); char *cx_to_ast (const char *in, char **err); // JSON AST char *cx_from_xml (const char *in, char **err); // reverse char *cx_from_json (const char *in, char **err); /* …yaml / toml / md / csv variants… */ Canonical and Identity c char *cx_canonical(const char *in, char **err); // strict canonical text char *cx_hash (const char *in, char **err); // SHA-256 hex int cx_eq (const char *a, const char *b, char **err); char *cx_diff (const char *a, const char *b, const char *fmt, char **err); Binary Forms c /* ast-bin — versioned binary AST */ char *cx_to_ast_bin (const char *in, size_t *out_len, char **err); char *cx_from_ast_bin (const char *buf, size_t buf_len, char **err); /* data-bin — Arrow / Parquet bridge (via libcx_arrow) */ char *cx_to_data_bin (const char *in, size_t *out_len, char **err); Include Resolution c char *cx_to_cx_with_include_root (const char *in, const char *root, char **err); char *cx_to_ast_bin_with_include_root (const char *in, const char *root, size_t *out_len, char **err); CXL Evaluator c char *cx_eval (const char *input, const char *program, const char *target, char **err); char *cx_eval_with_include_root (const char *input, const char *program, const char *target, const char *root, char **err); Schema Validation c char *cx_validate (const char *doc, const char *schema, const char *mode, const char *fail_on, char **err); Capability Bits cx_features returns a 32-bit bitfield naming the capabilities the linked libcx supports. Each capability has a stable bit; bindings query before calling new symbols. c unsigned int cx_features(void); Threading and GC libcx uses Boehm GC under the hood. Each calling thread must register before its first call and unregister at exit. Tier-1 bindings handle this automatically; embedders calling the C ABI directly should pair every thread setup with a tear-down. c void cx_init(void); // process-once initialization void cx_thread_register(void); // per-thread entry void cx_thread_unregister(void); // per-thread exit Memory Every char-pointer returned by libcx is heap-allocated. Call cx_free to release it. c void cx_free(void *p); Header File The dist/include/cx.h header is generated alongside the libcx build and ships with the release tarball. The header is the normative source of truth for the surface; this page tracks the same set in prose. Related All bindings CLI — the command-line equivalent of every C-ABI call"},{"slug":"bindings/csharp","title":"C-sharp Binding","summary":"NuGet: Cx.Lang. P/Invoke wrapper for .NET 8+.","text":"C-sharp Binding C-sharp Binding NuGet: Cx.Lang. P/Invoke wrapper for .NET 8+. Install shell dotnet add package Cx.Lang Parse and Emit csharp using Cx; var input = File.ReadAllText(\"pizza.cx\"); var json = CxApi.Emit(input, Format.Json); var xml = CxApi.Emit(input, Format.Xml); var tree = CxApi.Parse(input); foreach (var pizza in tree.FindAll(\"pizza\")) { Console.WriteLine($\"{pizza.Attr<string>(\\\"name\\\")} @ {pizza.Attr<decimal>(\\\"price\\\")}\"); } Run a CXL Template csharp var output = CxEval.Run(programText) .WithData(dataText) .WithIncludeRoot(\"/proj\") .Execute(); Type Mapping CX type to .NET type: sized integers to sbyte / short / int / long (signed) and byte / ushort / uint / ulong (unsigned); bigint to System.Numerics.BigInteger; decimal to System.Decimal; float to double (or float for f32); bool to bool; string to string; date to DateOnly; datetime to DateTimeOffset; bytes to byte[]. Related C ABI Binding catalog"},{"slug":"bindings/go","title":"Go Binding","summary":"go get github.com/cx-home/cx/lang/go. cgo wrapper, idiomatic Go API.","text":"Go Binding Go Binding go get github.com/cx-home/cx/lang/go. cgo wrapper, idiomatic Go API. Install shell go get github.com/cx-home/cx/lang/go Parse and Emit go import \"github.com/cx-home/cx/lang/go/cx\" tree, err := cx.Parse(input) if err != nil { return err } out, err := cx.Emit(tree, cx.JSON) xml, err := cx.Emit(tree, cx.XML) yaml, err := cx.Emit(tree, cx.YAML) Run a CXL Template go out, err := cx.Eval(programText, cx.WithData(dataText), cx.WithIncludeRoot(\"/proj\"), cx.WithTarget(cx.TargetHTML), ) Tree Access go tree, _ := cx.Parse(\"[shop [pizza name=Margherita price=12]]\") shop := tree.Element(\"shop\") pizza := shop.Find(\"pizza\")[0] name := pizza.Attr(\"name\").String() price := pizza.Attr(\"price\").Int() Schema Validation go diags, err := cx.Validate(doc, schema, cx.WithMode(cx.ModeStrict), cx.WithFailOn(cx.SeverityWarn), ) for _, d := range diags { fmt.Printf(\"%s %s at %s\\\\n\", d.Severity, d.Message, d.Path) } Streaming go it, _ := cx.Stream(reader) for it.Next() { if e := it.Event(); e.Kind == cx.ElementStart && e.Name == \"error\" { fmt.Println(e.Attrs) } } cgo and Thread Safety The Go binding uses cgo internally. Each goroutine that calls into libcx gets a thread registration on first use; the binding manages the Boehm GC handshake at every FFI chokepoint, so calling code does not need to think about it. Stateless format conversions are safe to call concurrently on disjoint inputs. Related C ABI — the layer this binding wraps Binding catalog"},{"slug":"bindings/index","title":"Bindings","summary":"Ten implementations, one libcx C ABI underneath. Pick your language.","text":"Bindings Bindings Ten implementations, one libcx C ABI underneath. Pick your language. Shape Every CX binding wraps the libcx C ABI. The API surface a binding exposes maps one-to-one with the underlying C entry point, so the shape you learn in one language carries to the next. Tier-1 bindings V Python Go gate every release feature; Tier-2 Rust C-sharp Java catches up once per release; Tier-3 TypeScript Kotlin Swift Ruby V-cffi lags by one minor release. Languages V — the native reference implementation C ABI — the symbols every binding calls Python Go Rust TypeScript Java C-sharp Ruby Kotlin Swift Parity Matrix Every binding implements the core capability set: parse, emit, format conversion CX with XML JSON YAML TOML Markdown, canonical form, hash, equality, ast-bin and data-bin one-shots, evaluator (cx eval), include resolution, and schema validation. Per-binding ergonomic conveniences differ; the underlying capabilities do not."},{"slug":"bindings/java","title":"Java Binding","summary":"Maven Central: io.cx-lang:cx. JNA wrapper, Java 17+.","text":"Java Binding Java Binding Maven Central: io.cx-lang:cx. JNA wrapper, Java 17+. Install (Maven) xml <dependency> <groupId>io.cx-lang</groupId> <artifactId>cx</artifactId> <version>0.7.0</version> </dependency> Install (Gradle) kotlin implementation('io.cx-lang:cx:0.7.0') Parse and Emit java import io.cxlang.Cx; import io.cxlang.Format; String input = Files.readString(Path.of(\"pizza.cx\")); String json = Cx.emit(input, Format.JSON); String xml = Cx.emit(input, Format.XML); var tree = Cx.parse(input); for (var pizza : tree.findAll(\"pizza\")) { System.out.println(pizza.attr(\"name\") + \" @ \" + pizza.attr(\"price\")); } Run a CXL Template java import io.cxlang.Eval; String out = Eval.run(programText) .withData(dataText) .withIncludeRoot(\"/proj\") .execute(); Schema Validation java import io.cxlang.Validate; var diagnostics = Validate.run(doc, schema) .withMode(Validate.Mode.STRICT) .execute(); Type Mapping CX type to Java type: int sized variants to byte / short / int / long (signed) or int / long / BigInteger (unsigned wider widths); bigint to BigInteger; decimal to BigDecimal; float / f64 / f32 to double / float; bool to boolean; string to String; date to LocalDate; datetime to OffsetDateTime; bytes to byte[]. Related C ABI Binding catalog"},{"slug":"bindings/kotlin","title":"Kotlin Binding","summary":"JNA wrapper. Idiomatic Kotlin DSL on top of the Java surface.","text":"Kotlin Binding Kotlin Binding JNA wrapper. Idiomatic Kotlin DSL on top of the Java surface. Install kotlin implementation('io.cx-lang:cx-kotlin:0.7.0') Parse and Emit kotlin import io.cxlang.kotlin.Cx import io.cxlang.kotlin.Format val input = java.io.File(\"pizza.cx\").readText() val json = Cx.emit(input, Format.JSON) val xml = Cx.emit(input, Format.XML) val tree = Cx.parse(input) tree.findAll(\"pizza\").forEach { pizza -> val name = pizza.attr<String>(\"name\") val price = pizza.attr<Double>(\"price\") println(\"$name @ $price\") } Run a CXL Template kotlin val out = Cx.eval(program) { data = dataText includeRoot = \"/proj\" } Related Java binding (shared JVM core) Binding catalog"},{"slug":"bindings/python","title":"Python Binding","summary":"pip install cx-lang. ctypes under the hood. Pythonic API on top.","text":"Python Binding Python Binding pip install cx-lang. ctypes under the hood. Pythonic API on top. Install shell pip install cx-lang # Or from source: git clone https://github.com/cx-home/cx cd cx/lang/python pip install -e . Parse and Emit python import cx tree = cx.parse(open('pizza.cx').read()) print(cx.emit(tree, to='json')) print(cx.emit(tree, to='yaml')) print(cx.canonical(open('pizza.cx').read())) Tree Access python tree = cx.parse('[shop name=Pepe [pizza name=Margherita price=12]]') # Pythonic: attributes look like dict; children look like a list shop = tree[0] print(shop['name']) # 'Pepe' print(shop[0]['name']) # 'Margherita' print(shop[0]['price']) # 12 (an int, not a string) # Iterate children for pizza in tree.find('pizza'): print(pizza['name'], pizza['price']) Run a CXL Template python import cx program = open('receipt.cxl').read() data = open('order.cx').read() print(cx.eval(program, data=data)) # With include resolution print(cx.eval(program, data=data, include_root='/proj/templates')) Format Conversion python yaml_text = open('config.yaml').read() cx_text = cx.from_yaml(yaml_text) # Or one-shot out = cx.convert(yaml_text, from_='yaml', to='cx') Schema Validation python diagnostics = cx.validate( open('order.cx').read(), schema=open('order.cxs').read(), mode='strict', ) for d in diagnostics: print(d.severity, d.message, d.path) Streaming python with open('events.cx', 'rb') as f: for event in cx.stream(f): if event.kind == 'element_start' and event.name == 'error': print(event.attrs) with cx.stream_writer(open('out.cx', 'wb')) as w: for record in fetch_records(): w.write(record) Type Mapping CX type to Python type: int to int, bigint to int, decimal to decimal.Decimal, float / f32 / f64 to float, bool to bool, string to str, date to datetime.date, datetime to datetime.datetime, bytes to bytes, null to None. Related C ABI — the layer this binding wraps Binding catalog"},{"slug":"bindings/ruby","title":"Ruby Binding","summary":"gem install cx-lang. FFI wrapper around libcx.","text":"Ruby Binding Ruby Binding gem install cx-lang. FFI wrapper around libcx. Install shell gem install cx-lang Parse and Emit ruby require \"cx\" input = File.read(\"pizza.cx\") json = Cx.emit(input, to: :json) xml = Cx.emit(input, to: :xml) tree = Cx.parse(input) tree.find_all(\"pizza\").each do |pizza| puts \"#{pizza['name']} @ #{pizza['price']}\" end Run a CXL Template ruby out = Cx.eval(program_text, data: data_text, include_root: \"/proj\") Type Mapping CX type to Ruby type: int / sized integers to Integer; bigint to Integer; decimal to BigDecimal; float to Float; bool to true and false; string to String; date to Date; datetime to DateTime; bytes to String with Encoding::ASCII_8BIT. Related C ABI Binding catalog"},{"slug":"bindings/rust","title":"Rust Binding","summary":"cargo add cx-lang. extern C wrapper, typed AST nodes.","text":"Rust Binding Rust Binding cargo add cx-lang. extern C wrapper, typed AST nodes. Install shell cargo add cx-lang Parse and Emit rust use cx_lang::{parse, emit, canonical, Format}; let tree = parse(&input)?; let json = emit(&tree, Format::Json)?; let xml = emit(&tree, Format::Xml)?; let canon = canonical(&input)?; Typed AST Access rust let tree = parse(\"[shop [pizza name=Margherita price=12]]\")?; for pizza in tree.find_all(\"pizza\") { let name: &str = pizza.attr(\"name\")?.as_str()?; let price: f64 = pizza.attr(\"price\")?.as_f64()?; println!(\"{} @ {}\", name, price); } Run a CXL Template rust use cx_lang::eval; let out = eval(program_text) .with_data(data_text) .with_include_root(\"/proj\") .with_target(Target::Html) .run()?; Schema Validation rust use cx_lang::{validate, Mode, Severity}; let diags = validate(doc, schema) .mode(Mode::Strict) .fail_on(Severity::Warn) .run()?; for d in diags { eprintln!(\"{:?} {} at {}\", d.severity, d.message, d.path); } Threading The Rust binding registers each calling thread with libcx via ensure_thread at every FFI chokepoint. cx_thread_register and cx_thread_unregister fire automatically; manual setup is unnecessary. Stateless conversions can be called from any thread without coordination. Type Mapping CX type to Rust type: int to i64 (or i8 / i16 / i32 / u8 / u16 / u32 / u64 if sized), bigint to num_bigint::BigInt, decimal to rust_decimal::Decimal, float to f64 (or f32 if sized), bool to bool, string to String, date to chrono::NaiveDate, datetime to chrono::DateTime, bytes to Vec u8. Related C ABI — the layer this binding wraps Binding catalog"},{"slug":"bindings/swift","title":"Swift Binding","summary":"Swift Package Manager. clang interop, native Swift API.","text":"Swift Binding Swift Binding Swift Package Manager. clang interop, native Swift API. Install swift // Package.swift dependencies: [ .package(url: \"https://github.com/cx-home/cx-swift\", from: \"0.7.0\"), ] Parse and Emit swift import Cx let input = try String(contentsOfFile: \"pizza.cx\") let json = try Cx.emit(input, to: .json) let xml = try Cx.emit(input, to: .xml) let tree = try Cx.parse(input) for pizza in tree.findAll(\"pizza\") { print(\"\\\\(pizza[\"name\"]) @ \\\\(pizza[\"price\"])\") } Run a CXL Template swift let out = try Cx.eval(program, data: dataText, includeRoot: \"/proj\") Related C ABI Binding catalog"},{"slug":"bindings/typescript","title":"TypeScript Binding","summary":"npm install @cx-lang/cxlib. koffi wrapper around libcx for Node and Bun.","text":"TypeScript Binding TypeScript Binding npm install @cx-lang/cxlib. koffi wrapper around libcx for Node and Bun. Install shell npm install @cx-lang/cxlib Parse and Emit ts import { parse, emit, canonical } from \"@cx-lang/cxlib\" const tree = parse(await Bun.file(\"pizza.cx\").text()) const json = emit(tree, { to: \"json\" }) const xml = emit(tree, { to: \"xml\" }) const c = canonical(await Bun.file(\"pizza.cx\").text()) Run a CXL Template ts import { evalCxl } from \"@cx-lang/cxlib\" const out = evalCxl(programText, { data: dataText, includeRoot: \"/proj\", target: \"html\", }) Runtime Notes The TS binding works under Node.js (18+) and Bun. Both use koffi for the C ABI handshake. The libcx Boehm GC settings GC_DONT_GC GC_INITIAL_HEAP_SIZE=32MB are applied before koffi.load so the mark phase under Node and Bun is bounded. For long-running processes a worker-recycle policy is recommended; the trade-off is documented in the binding README. Related C ABI Binding catalog"},{"slug":"bindings/v","title":"V Binding","summary":"V is the language libcx itself is written in. Direct calls, no FFI overhead.","text":"V Binding V Binding V is the language libcx itself is written in. Direct calls, no FFI overhead. Install shell vpm install cx-lang Parse and Emit v import cx doc := cx.parse(input) or { panic(err) } out := cx.emit_cx(doc) xml := cx.to_xml(input)! json := cx.to_json(input)! Run a CXL Template v import cx out := cx.eval_cxl(input_cx, program_cx, '') or { panic(err) } println(out) Includes v import cx doc := cx.parse_with_include_root(input, '/proj') or { panic(err) } out := cx.eval_cxl_with_include_root(input, program, '', '/proj') or { panic(err) } Canonical Form and Identity v canonical := cx.cx_text_canonical(input)! hash := cx.cx_text_hash(input)! eq := cx.cx_text_eq(a, b)! V Native Variant The V binding ships in two shapes. The native variant (lang/v/native) calls into the cx package directly with no FFI — zero overhead, suitable for builds that statically link libcx. The cffi variant (lang/v/cffi) wraps the C ABI through V is C interop, useful when consuming a prebuilt libcx.dylib or libcx.so from a separate process. Related C ABI — the layer V exposes to other bindings Binding catalog"},{"slug":"comparison","title":"Comparison: CX vs JSON, YAML, TOML, XML","summary":"Honest assessment of where CX wins and where another format is the right pick.","text":"Comparison: CX vs JSON, YAML, TOML, XML Comparison: CX vs JSON, YAML, TOML, XML Honest assessment of where CX wins and where another format is the right pick. Who This Is For This document is for people deciding whether to adopt CX. It compares CX to the four formats it converts to and from, with honest assessments of when CX is the right pick and when one of the others is a better fit. There is no need to choose CX for everything; CX is designed to live alongside these formats and convert losslessly between them. At a Glance CX JSON YAML TOML XML Syntax weight brackets, no closing tags curly braces + brackets indent-significant tables + key=val open + close tags Strong types ✅ int / float / bool / null / sized / decimal / bigint / date / datetime / bytes ❌ number only partial (auto-detect) ✅ int / float / bool / datetime partial (xs:type) Comments ✅ block and line ❌ ✅ # ... ✅ # ... ✅ Mixed content ✅ first-class ❌ ❌ ❌ ✅ first-class Multiple top-level docs ✅ no wrapper required ❌ requires array ✅ via --- separator ❌ single document partial Attribute / element distinction ✅ explicit ❌ flat keys ❌ flat keys ❌ flat keys ✅ explicit Type fidelity through round-trip ✅ via CXDB ❌ int↔float coerced partial ✅ preserved partial Tabular data efficiency ✅ :table block, columnar binary ❌ verbose array-of-objects ❌ verbose partial (array of tables) ❌ verbose Streaming parser ✅ pull-based handle API partial ❌ usually whole-file ❌ ✅ SAX The advantages buy you something concrete: type fidelity for typed config, mixed content and structured data in one file, lossless six-way conversion, and a stable canonical form for hashing and signing. Conversion-Loss Matrix Lossless conversion means the data round-trips without semantic loss; presentation details such as comments, whitespace, and attribute order may be normalized. The table below is the honest accounting of what survives, what gets normalized, and what is genuinely lossy. CX → format → CX Lossless? What Is Preserved What Is Normalized or Lost CX → JSON → CX data ✅ / presentation 📋 Element shape, attributes, types via CXDB, values, nesting Comments dropped; element vs attribute distinction collapses to keys CX → YAML → CX data ✅ / presentation 📋 Element shape, types via !!tags, multiline strings Comments dropped by most parsers; flow-vs-block style normalized CX → TOML → CX data ✅ / presentation 📋 Element shape via table headers, strong types Comments dropped; section ordering normalized; deep nesting flattened CX → XML → CX data ✅ / presentation 📋 Element shape, attributes, mixed content, namespaces, PIs Whitespace normalized per xml:space; entity references resolved CX → Markdown → CX data 📋 / presentation 📋 Headings, paragraphs, lists, emphasis, code, links Data-shaped elements flatten to inline text CX → CXDB → CX bytes ✅ Everything byte-for-byte Nothing — CXDB is the strict-canonical form CX → CSV → CX data partial :table block columns and rows Non-table data not encodable; cell subtyping collapses What Presentation Means Presentation-layer differences that conversions normalize away. Comments — only XML and CX preserve them; JSON has none; YAML and TOML lose them through most parsers Whitespace — indentation, blank lines, line endings normalized to the target conventions Attribute order — emission may pick a canonical order Anchor names — canonical form renumbers them These all show up as identical hashes via cx hash because the hash operates on strict-canonical bytes, where presentation is stripped. When CX Wins CX is the right pick for typed configuration, mixed content alongside structured data, lossless conversion across formats, and cases where you need a stable canonical form for hashing or signing. The other formats remain better picks for narrower use cases — JSON for browser-native parsing, YAML for human-curated config where indentation matters, TOML for flat single-file config, XML for document-shaped data with namespaces and processing instructions. Related Reading Format Co"},{"slug":"concepts/cxpath","title":"CXPath","summary":"The query language inside CXL directives — XPath-shaped, CX-native.","text":"CXPath CXPath The query language inside CXL directives — XPath-shaped, CX-native. What CXPath Is CXPath is the query language CXL uses inside directives. Where XPath queries an XML tree, CXPath queries a CXDM tree. The surface is familiar to anyone who has written XPath — paths, predicates, axes, and a substantial XPath 3.1 and 4.0 function library all available. Paths Paths look like Unix paths but walk the document tree. Double-slash matches any depth; single slash walks a direct step; the at-sigil prefixes an attribute lookup. cx //pizza # all pizza elements at any depth //menu/pizza # pizza elements that are direct children of menu //pizza/@name # attribute name of every pizza //pizza/topping/@name # walk down two steps, then read an attribute Predicates Square brackets after a step filter the matched set. Predicates can compare values, test attribute existence, call functions, use position, or compose with boolean operators. cx //pizza[@price > 10] //item[count(line) > 3] //user[starts-with(@id, 'admin-')] //*[local-name() = 'pizza'] //product[@stock > 0 and @price < 20] Axes An axis selects nodes by relationship to the context. CX supports the standard XPath axes; the most commonly used are highlighted below. cx child::pizza descendant::topping ancestor::menu parent::* self::* following-sibling::* preceding-sibling::* attribute::* Function Library The XPath function library is substantial. CXPath ships the working subset of XPath 3.1 and 4.0; common members: count, sum, max, min, position, last, string-length, substring, contains, starts-with, ends-with, lower-case, upper-case, normalize-space, local-name, name, namespace-uri. cx //item[@count = max(//item/@count)] [?= string-length(//page/@title)] //user[contains(@email, '@example.com')] Operators CXPath includes operators beyond comparison: arithmetic plus minus times divide mod, boolean and or not, set union intersect except, and the range operator (to). cx [?for n :in 1 to 10 :return [slot id=[?= n]]] //pizza | //side # union: pizzas and sides //item[@price * 1.08 > 20] # price plus tax > 20 Variables Variables introduced by ?for or ?let are referenced with the dollar-sign sigil. Most expressions allow the bare variable name; the dollar-sign form is required when the context is ambiguous collection-slot text predicate RHS. cx [?for it :in //item :return [?for ex :in //example :where ex/@id = it/@id :return [match item-id=[?= it/@id]]]] Related CXL — directives that embed CXPath CXPath function reference 50 Ways to Love Your CXL Code — CXPath in context"},{"slug":"concepts/data-model","title":"Data Model (CXDM)","summary":"One tree shape under every dialect. Elements, attributes, body items, structural slots.","text":"Data Model (CXDM) Data Model (CXDM) One tree shape under every dialect. Elements, attributes, body items, structural slots. One Tree, Six Surfaces CXDM is the shape every CX parse produces and every emit walks. Whether the source is CX, XML, JSON, YAML, TOML, or Markdown, the parser yields a tree in this single shape; whether the target is any of those six dialects, the emitter walks the same tree back out. Downstream code never branches on input format because the data model has already absorbed the difference. The Element A CXDM node is an Element with a name, an ordered list of attributes, an ordered list of body items, and a few optional structural slots — anchor, merge target, declared id, data type. Body items are Text nodes, Scalar nodes, child Elements, or the collection literals from ADR 0017 — Sequences, Arrays, Maps. cx [pizza #margherita :large size=14 crust=thin [topping cheese] [topping basil] Stone-baked since 1987.] Reading this element from the outside in: name pizza, declared id margherita, data-type annotation :large, attributes size=14 and crust=thin, then a body of three items — two child elements and a text node. Attributes Attributes are name=value pairs after the element name. Values can be bare, single-quoted, double-quoted, or triple-quoted. Bare values are auto-typed: 8080 becomes int, 3.14 becomes float, true becomes bool, 2026-01-01 becomes date. Leading-zero tokens stay strings — 02134 stays the string 02134 — to avoid the YAML Norway problem. Body Items in Order A body holds Text, Scalar, child Element, or collection literal in source order. Mixed content is first-class: text and structure can interleave the way they do in XML and Markdown, the way JSON cannot. cx [note Try the [b Margherita] — our [em best seller], hand-tossed daily.] Collection Literals ADR 0017 brings three collection shapes as first-class body and attribute values. Sequence with parens flattens; array with brackets preserves nesting; map with braces gives named key-to-value entries. cx [pizza toppings=(cheese, basil, oil)] [grid cells=[[1,0,1],[0,1,0],[1,0,1]]] [pizza prices={small: 9, medium: 12, large: 15}] Structural Slots Beyond name, attributes, and body, an element carries optional structural slots. Each slot is signalled by a sigil; each carries a single value or reference. Anchor (ampersand) — names this element so others can refer to or merge from it Merge target (asterisk) — copies attributes and children from a named anchor Declared id (hash) — stable identifier within scope; targets of references Data type (colon) — pins the body or value type, overriding auto-typing Whitespace Significance Whitespace inside a body is significant when it sits between text characters and collapses to a single space when it separates body items. The canonical form normalises this so byte-identical content carries the same hash regardless of source author choice. Related Sigils & Markup Identity & Canonical Form Types Grammar (EBNF)"},{"slug":"concepts/evaluation","title":"Evaluation and CXPath","summary":"Directives turn CX into a program; CXPath is the query language they run against the tree.","text":"Evaluation and CXPath Evaluation and CXPath Directives turn CX into a program; CXPath is the query language they run against the tree. Directives Turn Data Into a Language A directive is a CX element whose name starts with a question mark. It carries semantics — iteration, branching, substitution, include, function call — that the evaluator interprets when running a CXL program. Directives parse as regular CX, evaluate to text or values, and compose with each other through their slot bodies. cx [?= EXPR] # substitute a value [?for x :in PATH :return BODY] # iterate [?if [COND, THEN, ELSE]] # branch [?let x :be EXPR :return BODY] # bind a name [?def name :params [a, b] :body BODY] # define a template CXPath: Querying the Tree Anywhere a directive needs to point at a node or compute a value from one, the slot is a CXPath expression. CXPath looks like XPath — slashes for steps, the at-sigil for attributes, square brackets for predicates, axes for navigation by relationship. cx //page/section/@id //item[@price > 100]/@name //pizza[size = 'large'] parent::* descendant::topping ancestor::menu Predicates and the Function Library Predicates filter steps. Comparisons, name tests, position, and the XPath function library are all available. cx //item[position() = 1] //*[local-name() = 'pizza'] //order[count(items) > 3] //user[starts-with(@id, 'admin-')] Context and Scoping Every CXL directive runs with a current context — by default the input document root. A bare path like //order finds order elements anywhere in the document; a relative path like item resolves against the current context. The ?for and ?let directives introduce named bindings. Inside the body, the binding shadows any outer binding of the same name. The ?with directive shifts the context for its body. cx [?for o :in //order :return [?let n :be o/items :return [order id=[?= o/@id] count=[?= count(n)]]]] [?with //order/items :return [?for it :in . :return [line [?= it/@name]]]] Related CXL reference — full directive list 50 ways to write a CXL program"},{"slug":"concepts/identity","title":"Identity and Canonical Form","summary":"Hash the canonical bytes. Declared ids and references on top.","text":"Identity and Canonical Form Identity and Canonical Form Hash the canonical bytes. Declared ids and references on top. Two Layers of Identity Identity in CX has two layers. The first is content-addressed — every tree has a SHA-256 over its canonical bytes; two trees with the same data have the same hash. The second is declarational — an element can carry an id via the hash sigil, and other places in the document can reference it via the at-sigil. Canonical Form The canonical mode strips presentation — comments, anchors, whitespace, attribute order normalisation — and emits a data-equivalent byte-identical form. Two documents with the same data emit to the same bytes; the difference between them is something a human added, not something the data carries. shell cx canonical menu.cx # canonical bytes cx hash menu.cx # SHA-256 hex over canonical bytes cx eq a.cx b.cx # exit 0 iff canonical(a) == canonical(b) Canonical form is the basis for content-addressed identity (cx hash), for byte-identical conformance tests across bindings, and for diff that ignores presentation. If two trees should be considered equal, canonical is what compares them. Declared Identifiers Declared identity is what you write when you want one element to point at another by name. Declare with the hash sigil; reference with the at-sigil. IDs are unique within their scope; the scope is the document root by default. cx [order #main [item #latte] [item #scone]] [receipt order=@main] Anchors and Merge Targets An anchor (ampersand sigil) names a value once and lets later places refer to it without repeating it. A merge target (asterisk sigil) folds the anchor referent into the current element. Anchors are anonymous handles; the canonical form renumbers them so two documents that bind the same data with different anchor names hash to the same identity. cx [defaults &shared timeout=30 retries=3] [server *shared name=api] [server *shared name=worker] ast-bin and CXDB ast-bin is the binary serialization of the canonical form. CXDB is the on-disk byte-stable archive of a CX document. Both round-trip byte-for-byte with the text form; both are versioned via capability bits on the C ABI. Related Format conversion — what canonical preserves and what it normalises Identity guarantees vs other formats"},{"slug":"concepts/sigils","title":"Sigils and Markup","summary":"Five single-character markers that give CX its structural reach beyond name and attribute.","text":"Sigils and Markup Sigils and Markup Five single-character markers that give CX its structural reach beyond name and attribute. The Catalog CX uses five sigils. Each is a single character in a fixed lexical position that gives a value structural meaning beyond what bare text can carry. at-sigil — attribute prefix in body position; reference to a declared id or anchor ampersand — anchor declaration; names an element so others can refer to or merge from it asterisk — merge target; folds the named anchors attrs and children into this element hash — declared id; stable handle within the documents scope; targets of references colon — data type annotation; pins the value type, overriding auto-typing At-Sigil — Attributes and References The at-sigil does double duty. In CXPath expressions and in attribute-value position, @name refers to the attribute named name. In element-body position, @target is a reference to an anchor or declared id. cx [order #o1234 customer=alice [line item=@margherita count=2] # at-sigil reference to declared id [line item=@pepperoni count=1]] [?for line :in //order/line :return [row sku=[?= line/@item]]] # at-sigil attribute access in CXPath Ampersand — Anchors An anchor names a value once and lets later places refer to it without repeating it. cx [defaults &shared timeout=30 retries=3] [server *shared name=api] [server *shared name=worker] Asterisk — Merge Targets Merge folds the named anchors attrs and children into the current element. Anchored attributes and children come first; locally declared attrs and children override or extend them. The canonical form renumbers anchors so two documents that bind the same data with different anchor names hash to the same identity. Hash — Declared IDs An id declares this element as a target of references. IDs are unique within their scope; the scope is the document root by default. References use the at-sigil. cx [order #main [item #latte] [item #scone]] [receipt order=@main] Colon — Data Type Annotations A colon-type annotation pins the value or body type. Sized integer variants :i8 :i16 :i32 :i64 :u8 :u16 :u32 :u64 carry range constraints that validate at parse time. Floating point variants :f32 :f64 and arbitrary-precision :decimal :bigint pick the runtime representation. cx [port :u16 8080] # rejects values over 65535 at parse time [ratio :decimal 0.30000] # exact decimal, not IEEE 754 [temp :int 0xFFAA] # hex literal pinned as int Boolean Sigils Two additional sigils — plus and minus — are syntactic sugar for boolean attributes. Plus-name parses as name=true; minus-name parses as name=false. Compact form for many booleans. cx [pizza +vegan -nuts -gluten +organic] Markup: Mixed Content CX inherits XMLs first-class mixed content. Inside an element body, text and child elements interleave freely. The same shapes that compose an HTML document compose a CX document: h1, p, em, strong, a, etc. cx [article [h1 New Haven Story] [p Founded in [em 1987]. See the [a href=/menu menu].] [p Still [strong hand-tossing] every pie.]] Related Data Model — where sigils attach Identity — hash, ref-by-id, canonical renumbering Types — what colon-type pins"},{"slug":"concepts/types","title":"Types","summary":"CXs type system: auto-typed values, sized annotations, decimal and bigint, and the lossless round-trip.","text":"Types Types CXs type system: auto-typed values, sized annotations, decimal and bigint, and the lossless round-trip. Atomic Types CX values fall into a small set of atomic types — int, float, bool, string, null, date, datetime, bytes, decimal, bigint — and arrays of any of these. Most values are auto-typed from their lexical shape; explicit annotations cover the rest. Auto-Typing Bare values are auto-typed when their lexical shape is unambiguous. cx [demo count=42 # int pi=3.14 # float enabled=true # bool name=Alice # string missing=null # null date=2026-05-09 # date start=2026-05-09T10:00:00Z] # datetime Leading-Zero Strings Tokens with a leading zero stay strings unless prefixed 0x (hex), 0o (octal), or 0b (binary). Postal codes, area codes, account numbers, BIC codes — anywhere the leading zero is data, not a numeric value. cx [zip 02134] # string \"02134\", not int 2134 [mask 0xFF00FF] # int 16711935 (hex) [perms 0o755] # int 493 (octal) [bits 0b1101] # int 13 (binary) Sized Integer and Float A colon-type pins the runtime representation and (for sized variants) the legal range. Out-of-range values fail at parse time, not at runtime in some downstream consumer. cx [port :u16 8080] # rejects values over 65535 [balance :i64 -1234567890] # signed 64-bit [ratio :f32 3.14] # 32-bit float [count :u8 200] # rejects 256 and up Decimal and BigInt IEEE 754 floats lose precision for money and high-precision quantities; signed 64-bit overflows for some IDs. CX has :decimal and :bigint for both cases, with binding mappings to each languages native big-number type. cx [total :decimal 19.99] [user-id :bigint 18446744073709551616] Typed Bodies A colon-type annotation pins not just attribute values but body content as well. cx [count :int 42] [ratio :decimal 3.14159] [oven-temps :int[] 220 240 260 480] [tags :[] cheap fast tasty] Typed Columnar Tables The :table block carries column types end-to-end. CSV export and re-import preserve the schema; ast-bin and data-bin store rows column-major, which is 5 to 10 times smaller than equivalent element-per-row encoding. cx [orders :table[item:string qty:u32 paid:bool when:date] Margherita 2 true 2026-05-09 Hawaiian 1 false 2026-05-09 ] Binding Type Mapping Each bindings README has the language-specific mapping. The shape is consistent: CX int to native int (sized variants when the language has them); decimal to native arbitrary-precision decimal Python Decimal Java BigDecimal .NET decimal Ruby BigDecimal; bigint to native arbitrary-precision int. Related Data Model Schemas — declared and validated types Tables — typed columnar data"},{"slug":"concepts/wasm","title":"WebAssembly Target","summary":"WASM is the long-term distribution target. C ABI is the current mechanism. The boundary moves, the contract stays.","text":"WebAssembly Target WebAssembly Target WASM is the long-term distribution target. C ABI is the current mechanism. The boundary moves, the contract stays. Why WebAssembly CX ships today as a native C ABI library — libcx.dylib on macOS, libcx.so on Linux — that every binding loads through its own foreign-function machinery ctypes for Python cgo for Go koffi for TypeScript P/Invoke for C# JNA for Java/Kotlin FFI gem for Ruby swift-c for Swift bindgen for Rust. It works and it is fast, but it carries two ongoing costs. Every supported triple needs a binary; every binding carries its own FFI plumbing. A WebAssembly target collapses both. The long-term distribution target is a single libcx.wasm artifact loaded by each binding through its host runtime — wasmtime, wasmer, the browser, Node, V8, Wasmer-Python. The function names, the binary wire protocol, and the per-language Document API all stay the same. The boundary moves; the contract stays. What the C ABI Gives Us Today libcx compiled to a platform-specific .dylib or .so include/cx.h exporting ~49 functions (the full surface) Per-language FFI binding (ctypes, cgo, extern \"C\", koffi, P/Invoke, JNA, FFI, swift-c) Platform binaries: macOS arm64 + Linux x86_64 today; per-triple expansion as bindings need them What Changes With WebAssembly The shape of the artifact and the loader change. The function surface does not. libcx compiles to libcx.wasm — one portable binary, no per-triple build matrix No per-language FFI setup; each binding loads the WASM module through the host runtime instead Memory crosses linear-memory boundaries: caller writes the input string into WASM linear memory, reads the output back out, calls cx_free in the module Same function names, same → char* contract — projected onto linear-memory offsets What Stays the Same The contract is what matters; the loader is implementation. Every function name and semantic — the ~49-entry surface in include/cx.h The ast-bin wire protocol — byte-identical, untouched by the loader change The data-bin format and Arrow C-Data ABI — same bytes, same chunked-column semantics The Document API in each binding — native data structures over the same wire bytes, unaffected The error contract per language — same conventions, same code surface Per-Binding Changes When WASM Lands Catch-up is a binding-level swap, not a rewrite. Four steps per binding. Replace the FFI loader (ctypes / cgo / koffi / P/Invoke / JNA / FFI gem / swift-c) with a WASM runtime loader for that language Replace direct memory pointers with read/write against the WASM linear-memory buffer Keep the binary decoders unchanged — ast-bin and data-bin payloads carry the same bytes Keep the Document API implementation unchanged — it operates on the decoded tree, not on the FFI boundary Status libcx-wasm is not yet built. The C ABI is the only distribution mechanism on v0.7.0; the WASM target is tracked for the v0.7.x window. A reference WASM build will land alongside a parity-tested rollout across the 10-binding matrix — until that point, bindings MUST link against the C ABI and SHOULD NOT assume the WASM artifact exists. The playgrounds and live-eval scenarios that reference libcx-wasm in their messaging are forward-looking; they fall back to canned outputs today. Track concrete progress in the GitHub repo. When libcx-wasm reaches a usable artifact, the Playground page swaps its lookup-table evaluator for the live module without changing the page UI. Related C ABI — the current distribution mechanism, full function list Install — how libcx is built and loaded today Playground — the first consumer of libcx-wasm once it ships Streaming — how memory crosses the boundary today"},{"slug":"cookbook","title":"Cookbook","summary":"Task-shaped recipes. I want to X — here is the X.","text":"Cookbook Cookbook Task-shaped recipes. I want to X — here is the X. Read This First Each recipe is a concrete task with the smallest CX or CXL that solves it. Browse the headings, find the one that sounds like what you are trying to do, copy-paste, and adapt. Each recipe links to the deeper reference where the underlying mechanism is documented. Convert YAML to JSON shell cx convert config.yaml --to=json > config.json Auto-detected by extension. Override with --from when the extension lies. Convert TOML to YAML shell cx convert config.toml --to=yaml > config.yaml Extract a Single Value shell cx eval -e '[?= //server/@host]' --data=config.cx The -e flag takes the template inline; --data is the input file. The CXPath finds the host attribute of any server element at any depth. Filter Records by Attribute Run cx eval -e with a CXL inline that filters by predicate. The CXPath predicate //user[@role='admin'] selects only admins. shell cx eval template.cxl --data=users.cx Count Records shell cx eval -e '[?= count(//order)]' --data=orders.cx Validate Against a Schema shell cx validate order.cx --schema=order.cxs cx validate order.cx --schema=order.cxs --mode=strict cx validate order.cx --schema=order.cxs --fail-on=warn Exit 0 if no diagnostics at or above fail-on, 1 if any, 2 on I/O or schema-load failure. Compute the Canonical Hash shell cx canonical menu.cx cx hash menu.cx cx eq a.cx b.cx Use the hash for content-addressing, cache keys, or signing. cx eq returns exit 0 iff canonical(a) equals canonical(b). Semantic Diff Between Two Documents shell cx diff old.cx new.cx # unified cx diff --format=json old.cx new.cx cx diff --format=summary old.cx new.cx The diff walks the canonical form so comment and whitespace changes do not show up. Lint a File shell cx lint config.cx cx lint config.cx --only=CX-L005 cx lint config.cx --disable=CX-L001,CX-L007 Format / Re-Indent shell cx fmt config.cx # in-place if you redirect cx fmt --check config.cx # exit non-zero if not canonical cx fmt < input.cx > output.cx Generate a Config From a Template cx [server host=[?= //env/@host] port=[?= //env/@port] workers=[?if [//env/@env = \"prod\", 32, 4]] cache=[?if [//env/@env = \"prod\", /var/cache/app, /tmp/cache]]] shell cx eval server.cxl --data=env.prod.cx --to=toml > server.toml Stream a Large File python import cx with open(\"events.cx\", \"rb\") as f: for event in cx.stream(f): if event.kind == \"element_start\" and event.name == \"error\": print(event.attrs) RSS stays bounded regardless of input size. Tier-1 bindings V Python Go ship the streaming reader and writer; Tier-2/3 follow in v0.7.x. Write a Stream python with cx.stream_writer(open(\"out.cx\", \"wb\")) as w: for record in fetch_records(): w.write(record) Compose a Document From Files cx [server [?cx include=lib/defaults.cx] port=8080 log-level=info] shell cx --cx --include-root=./conf server.cx > resolved.cx The parse-time include splices the contents of lib/defaults.cx in place. The eval-time variant is for dynamic includes inside CXL templates. Render a Template With Includes cx [?include 'partials/header.cxl'] [?for item :in //order/line :return [?include 'partials/line.cxl']] [?include 'partials/footer.cxl'] shell cx eval order.cxl --data=order.cx --include-root=./templates Emit a Table as CSV shell cx table dump orders.cx --to=csv > orders.csv cx table dump orders.cx --to=tsv > orders.tsv cx table dump orders.cx --to=psv > orders.psv The CSV header carries the column types so the reverse direction (cx table load) reconstructs them. Read a Parquet File Into CX shell cx table load orders.parquet --to=cx > orders.cx Phase A (Arrow round-trip) ships at v0.7.0; Phase B (Parquet writer) and Phase C (read-side schema inference) follow in v0.7.x. Use an ID-Based Reference cx [order #o1234 customer=alice [line item=@margherita count=2] [line item=@salad count=1]] [receipt order=@o1234] Hash declares an id; at-sigil references it. Identifiers are unique within the documents scope. Share Defaults Across Recor"},{"slug":"faq","title":"FAQ","summary":"Working answers to the questions adopters ask in the first thirty minutes.","text":"FAQ FAQ Working answers to the questions adopters ask in the first thirty minutes. Read This First Working answers for the questions adopters ask in the first thirty minutes. For the format itself see the Tutorial; for the formal grammar see the Grammar (EBNF) reference; for the comparison with other formats see the vs JSON / YAML / TOML / XML page. Why CX Instead of JSON? JSON has no comments and no type fidelity beyond IEEE 754 doubles. That is fine for API payloads but punishing for configuration (rationale belongs next to the values it explains) and for data exchange that involves big integers, decimals, dates, or anything outside the JSON value tower. CX keeps the round-trip: cx --json file.cx then cx --from json brings bigints, decimals, dates, and types back unchanged. JSON itself silently breaks any integer over two-to-the-53rd. Use JSON when the consumer is fixed and the data fits the JSON value tower. Use CX when the same data needs to round-trip into and out of multiple consumers without losing meaning. Why CX Instead of YAML? YAMLs implicit typing is the load-bearing problem: country: NO parses as false; version: 1.10 parses as 1.1; port: 022 parses as octal 18. CX inverts the rule: leading-zero tokens are strings (so 02134 stays the string 02134), colon-type annotations make the type explicit, and there is no implicit boolean coercion of strings. YAMLs significant whitespace is hostile to programmatic generation — a stray tab corrupts a deeply nested document. CX uses brackets, which programmatic emitters cannot accidentally break. Why CX Instead of TOML? TOML is excellent for flat, table-shaped configuration. It struggles with deep hierarchy: bracket-dot headers get repetitive, and mixing arrays-of-tables with inline tables leaks complexity. CX nests freely at any depth with no syntactic penalty, and exports to TOML losslessly when the source happens to fit TOMLs shape. Why CX Instead of XML? XML has the strongest schema and namespace story of any text format in wide use. It is also verbose: closing tags repeat, attribute quoting is mandatory, and the XML declaration and DOCTYPE machinery is cumbersome. CX gives you XMLs structural power attributes mixed content namespaces id/idref without the verbosity. A CX document that round-trips to XML is typically half the byte count. Why CX Instead of Markdown? Markdown is the right format for prose with light structure — articles, READMEs, blog posts. It has no opinion about types, schemas, or data. CX subsumes Markdown for the cases where prose and structured data live together. If you only need prose, stay in Markdown. When Is CX the Wrong Choice? Hot-path JSON for high-throughput APIs. Use JSON; the parsing ecosystem is faster and more universal. Kubernetes and the Helm ecosystem. Stay in YAML; the tooling expects it. Pure prose documents. Stay in Markdown. Database export with type-fidelity guarantees. Use Parquet or Arrow. Is CX Production-Ready? v0.7.0 is the API and format-stability boundary through 1.0. C ABI symbols are append-only from here forward; ast-bin format versions are forward-compatible; the cross-binding conformance suite gates every release. Adopt today and expect format stability through 1.0 with incremental tooling improvements. Can I Adopt Incrementally? Yes. Every CX file converts losslessly to one or more of the five formats it interoperates with. Use CX as your source of truth and emit YAML for Kubernetes, TOML for static-site configs, and Markdown for docs; or author in JSON / YAML / TOML and convert to CX when you need comments, types, or hierarchy. Which Language Can I Use CX From? Ten implementations, all backed by the same V core. Each binding wraps the libcx C ABI; the API shape differs idiomatically but the underlying capability set is the same. V — the native reference implementation Python — ctypes wrapper Go — cgo wrapper Rust — extern C wrapper TypeScript — koffi wrapper for Node Java — JNA wrapper Kotlin — JNA wrapper Swift — clang "},{"slug":"index","title":"CX — One Bracket for Every Data Format","summary":"A unified syntax for configuration, data, documents, prose, and programs — losslessly convertible to JSON, YAML, TOML, XML, and Markdown. Built for humans and the AI systems that work alongside them.","text":"CX — One Bracket for Every Data Format CX — One Bracket for Every Data Format A unified syntax for configuration, data, documents, prose, and programs — losslessly convertible to JSON, YAML, TOML, XML, and Markdown. Built for humans and the AI systems that work alongside them. Hello, CX The document below is one CX file. It carries typed configuration, structured data, prose with mixed-content markup, multi-line strings, declared identifiers, references, anchors with merge, a typed columnar table, and a CXL directive that templates a receipt — all in one syntax. The same parser handles every shape; the same evaluator can read, transform, and emit it as any of the six surface dialects CX speaks. cx [- ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── New Haven Pizza — one document covers every shape on the menu. Block comments wrap multi-line notes like this; line comments live outside element bodies with the hash sigil. ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -] # top-of-file header comment [?xml version=1.0 encoding=UTF-8] [shop &flagship name='New Haven Pizza' port=8080 +open # opening hours — bare attributes, auto-typed [hours mon-fri=11-22 sat=12-23 sun=closed] [- Typed columnar menu. Each column declares a type that round-trips end-to-end through CSV, Arrow, and Parquet. -] [menu :table[name:string size price:decimal vegan:bool] Margherita medium 12.00 true Hawaiian large 14.00 false Diavola medium 13.00 false] [- Anchor declares a shared shape; merge inherits with overrides. -] [staff [cook &lead name=Pepe shift=morning +founder] [cook *lead name=Maria shift=closing -founder]] [- Sigils and types on attribute values. -] [contact address='123 Forno Lane' coords={lat: 40.8518, lon: 14.2681} phones=('+39 555 0101', '+39 555 0102') open-since :date 1987-05-12] [- Mixed content — prose interleaves with markup the way HTML does. -] [about [p Founded in [em 1987]. Still [strong hand-tossing] every pie.] [p See the [a href=/menu menu] for tonights specials.]] [- Multi-line string — triple-quoted, verbatim. -] [tagline Hand-tossed since 1987. Still tossing.] [- A CXL directive inside the same document. cx eval reads the surrounding tree, walks //order/line, and emits a receipt row per line item — no separate template engine, no sprintf. -] [?for line :in //order/line :return [row sku=[?= line/@item] qty=[?= line/@count]]]] Eight concerns, one document, one syntax. The same tree parses and emits as XML, JSON, YAML, TOML, or Markdown; canonical-form bytes are content-addressed via SHA-256; the directive language is just data the evaluator interprets. What Makes CX Different Each row below is the answer to a real friction in existing formats. The Comparison page walks the same list with side-by-side examples. Six surface dialects, one tree — round-trip CX, JSON, YAML, TOML, XML, and Markdown without losing data Content-addressed identity — SHA-256 over canonical bytes; two trees with the same data have the same hash Evaluator built in — CXL directives are just CX, no separate template engine, no sprintf Five structural sigils — declared ids, references, anchors, merge targets, and data types as first-class syntax Typed columnar tables — sized integer, decimal, datetime survive end-to-end through CSV, Arrow, and Parquet Mixed content first-class — text and structure interleave in one body, the way XML and Markdown do, the way JSON cannot Include resolver — compose documents at parse time and at eval time, with cycle detection and lexical containment Streaming parse and emit — process gigabyte inputs with bounded RSS, callback-driven Built for Humans and AI Alike Modern systems are not just humans editing config files. Agentic LLMs, retrieval pipelines, evaluation harnesses, and multi-step tool-use loops all need to capture, transfer, transform, and emit data alongside prose. The two have lived in separate formats for decades — JSON for the structured side, Markdown for the prose side — which forces e"},{"slug":"install","title":"Install","summary":"Get the cx binary and any language binding. One section per platform.","text":"Install Install Get the cx binary and any language binding. One section per platform. The Command-Line Binary The cx binary carries the parser, the emitter, the evaluator, the schema validator, the LSP, and every conversion entry point. It runs everywhere with no runtime dependency. macOS shell brew install cx-lang/tap/cx The tap drops a universal binary on Apple Silicon and Intel; arm64 and x86_64 ship in the same bottle. Linux Homebrew works on Linux too. The static binary tarball from the GitHub Releases page works on any glibc-based distro. shell brew install cx-lang/tap/cx curl -L https://github.com/cx-home/cx/releases/latest/download/cx-linux-x86_64.tar.gz \\\\ | tar xz -C /usr/local/bin Windows A static cx.exe ships per release on github.com/cx-home/cx/releases. Drop it on PATH; no installer, no dependencies. WebAssembly The libcx-wasm bundle is on the v0.7.x roadmap and is what will power the live evaluator in the playground. It is not yet on a release tag; track progress in the GitHub repo. Build From Source shell git clone https://github.com/cx-home/cx cd cx devbox run -- make build-vcx devbox run -- make test The build chain expects V on PATH or the cx-home V fork at third_party/v; the Makefile auto-detects either. Language Bindings Each binding wraps libcx via the C ABI; the cx binary above includes libcx as part of its build. Per-language install commands live on the binding pages. V Python Go Rust TypeScript Java C-sharp Ruby Kotlin Swift Editors and LSP The cx binary speaks LSP via cx lsp; editor install scripts at tooling/install land the VS Code or Neovim integration. shell ./tooling/install/install-vscode.sh ./tooling/install/install-nvim.sh The full editor catalog and the tree-sitter grammar are on the Tooling pages. Verify the Install shell cx --version cx --json - <<< \"[hello world]\" A successful run prints the version and a JSON document on stdout. If either fails, the build needs libcx on the loader path LD_LIBRARY_PATH on Linux DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH on macOS. Where to Next Quickstart — five minutes from install to running output Tutorial — guided walk-through Editors and LSP — syntax highlighting, completions, diagnostics"},{"slug":"performance","title":"Performance","summary":"Numbers and the bench setup that produced them.","text":"Performance Performance Numbers and the bench setup that produced them. Parse Parse throughput on a single core at v0.7.0-dev — CX at roughly 380 MB/s, XML at 290 MB/s, JSON at 510 MB/s, YAML at 95 MB/s. Bench setup: M2 Pro, libcx built with -prod, 1 MB fixture per format. Emit Emit throughput follows parse — same order of magnitude per format. The canonical-form emitter pays an extra normalisation pass and runs around 60 percent of the regular emit. Eval The CXL streaming evaluator runs at 340 MB/s on the comparable bench corpus — about 17 percent under the comparable JSON benchmark on the same workload. Bench setup mirrors the parse row above: M2 Pro, libcx built with -prod, 1 MB fixture. The v0.7.0 target was 300 MB/s; that line has been crossed. Binding Overhead FFI overhead per call is in the low microseconds. Python ctypes adds about 35 microseconds per parse-and-emit round-trip on a 1 KB input; Go cgo is similar; Rust bindgen is closer to one microsecond. See the per-binding API pages for the exact wrappers. Methodology The bench is at vcx/tests/runners/streaming_bench.v and vcx/tests/runners/eval_features_bench.v. Run with make bench; results land in target/bench.json. The cross-binding parity check at scripts/run_bench_json.py compares per-binding times against the V baseline. Related FAQ Streaming surface"},{"slug":"playground","title":"Playground","summary":"","text":"Playground Playground Example CX 1. Atom — element with one attribute CX 2. Attributes — typed, sized, sigil-flagged CX 3. Sigils — id, anchor, merge, data-type CX 4. Typed table — orders CX 5. Anchor + merge — shared defaults CX 6. Mixed content — prose + markup CX 7. Multi-document — two menus in one file CXL 8. Substitute a value CXL 9. Iterate with [?for] CXL 10. Multi-branch [?if] CXL 11. Let — bind once, reuse CXL 12. Filter pipeline — trim + upper CXL 13. Named template — ?def + ?use CXL 14. CXPath — axes and predicates CXL 15. cx:merge — three-policy semantic merge CXL 16. Include — partial composition Run Reset Source cx copy Canonical CX JSON XML Browser-based libcx-wasm evaluator planned — see Concepts → WebAssembly Target for the transition plan. Until that ships, Run is disabled and the output panes carry pre-recorded canned results for each example. The Source pane is fully editable; use Reset to restore the canned source, or switch examples via the dropdown. How This Works The playground runs entirely in the browser. Pick a starter example from the dropdown, edit it in the Source pane, and the three output tabs underneath show the canonical CX form, the JSON projection, and the XML projection of the parsed tree. No server, no signup. Sixteen starter examples ship in the corpus today — seven CX shapes atom attributes sigils typed table anchor and merge mixed content multi-document and nine CXL snippets substitute ?for multi-branch ?if ?let filter pipeline named template CXPath axes and predicates cx:merge includes. Edit any of them in place; the Run button replays the example output today, and the v0.7.x WebAssembly build of libcx will route the same button through a live evaluator. Offline The browser playground is a static page. Clone the repo, render the doc tree with make docs, and serve _site/ from any HTTP server — the playground works without an internet connection. The example corpus, the TypeScript bundle, and the CSS all live under scripts/gen_docs/playground/. Syntax Highlighting Every code block on this site is syntax-highlighted by a tiny in-house JS module at scripts/gen_docs/highlight/. It speaks the languages the docs actually use — cx, cxl, shell, python, go, rust, javascript, typescript, java, c-sharp, ruby, kotlin, swift, v, sql, ebnf, xml, html, json, yaml, toml, md. The highlighter is regex-based, around 150 lines, no external library. The Playground panes use the same module on every Run. Where to Next 50 Ways to Love Your CX Data — more example shapes 50 Ways to Love Your CXL Code — more directives Install — run cx locally with the full evaluator Showcase — built-with-CX examples, including this doc-gen"},{"slug":"quickstart","title":"Quickstart","summary":"Five minutes from cx installed to your first parse, emit, and evaluator run.","text":"Quickstart Quickstart Five minutes from cx installed to your first parse, emit, and evaluator run. Setup This page assumes cx is on your PATH already; if not, the Install page covers each platform. The rest of Quickstart is a five-minute hands-on session. shell cx --version # confirms the install mkdir cx-tour && cd cx-tour Write a Document Save the following as menu.cx. Read it top to bottom — name, attributes, body items, nested children, mixed content. No commas required between attrs; no closing tag to repeat. cx [shop name='New Haven Pizza' open=true [hours mon-fri=11-22 sat=12-23 sun=closed] [menu [pizza #margherita size=large crust=thin price=14.50] [pizza #pepperoni size=large crust=thick price=16.00] [side #salad price=6.50]]] Parse shell cx --ast menu.cx # JSON view of the parsed tree cx --cx menu.cx # canonical CX (attribute order normalised) The parse never returns a partial tree; a syntax error names the dialect, the byte offset, and the rule that failed. Emit to Another Format The same tree converts to any of the six dialects CX speaks. Try them. shell cx --json menu.cx cx --xml menu.cx cx --yaml menu.cx cx --toml menu.cx cx --md menu.cx Each command writes the result to stdout. Round-trip via the binary form (cx --cxdb | cx --from=cxdb) is byte-stable; round-trip via JSON / YAML / TOML / XML / Markdown is data-equivalent, with presentation-level differences documented on the comparison page. Evaluate a CXL Template CXL is CX that does things. Save the following as receipt.cxl alongside menu.cx. cx [?for p :in //pizza :return [line [?= p/@name] - [?= p/@price] euros;]] shell cx eval receipt.cxl --data=menu.cx The result is one receipt line per pizza in the menu. The directive language uses CXPath to query the data; the Tour of 50 Programs walks through the full directive vocabulary. Compute the Canonical Hash shell cx canonical menu.cx cx hash menu.cx cx eq menu.cx some-other-menu.cx The canonical form strips presentation comments attribute order anchor names whitespace and emits a data-equivalent byte-identical form. The hash is SHA-256 over those bytes. Two documents with the same data have the same hash — in any dialect, in any binding. Call CX From Your Language Every binding mirrors the CLI. Python by way of example: python import cx tree = cx.parse(open(\"menu.cx\").read()) print(cx.emit(tree, to=\"json\")) The Bindings pages cover Python, Go, Rust, V, TypeScript, Java, C-sharp, Ruby, Kotlin, and Swift — each idiomatic, all backed by the same libcx. Where to Next Tutorial — guided walk-through, atoms to closures, with exercises 50 Ways to Love Your CX Data — short examples, simple to complex 50 Ways to Love Your CXL Code — CXL snippets, simple to complex Playground — try CX in the browser CLI reference"},{"slug":"reference/ast-bin","title":"ast-bin Binary AST","summary":"Versioned binary serialization of the CXDM tree. Used for cross-binding handoff and stable disk storage.","text":"ast-bin Binary AST ast-bin Binary AST Versioned binary serialization of the CXDM tree. Used for cross-binding handoff and stable disk storage. Purpose ast-bin is a compact, versioned binary serialization of the CXDM tree. It round-trips byte-for-byte with the canonical text form, runs at the FFI boundary between every binding and libcx, and forms the canonical-bytes baseline for the cross-binding conformance suite. Version v6 ships at v0.7.0; advertised by capability bit 29 on the libcx C ABI. Earlier versions v3 v4 v5 remain readable by libcx through the lifetime of v0.7.x. Bindings query cx_features before writing v6 payloads to confirm support on the linked libcx. CLI shell cx --ast menu.cx > menu.ast.json # binary form via the C ABI / per-binding API: # cx_to_ast_bin (encode) # cx_from_ast_bin (decode) Specification The wire format is documented in spec/ast_bin.md. Tag bytes 0x0F / 0x10 / 0x11 cover the ADR 0017 collection literals (Sequence / Array / Map). Body refs land at ast_bin v3; the v4-to-v6 increments cover schema-content-mismatch fields, validator metadata, and the typed-table compression added at v0.7.0. Related C ABI — the cx_to_ast_bin entry points data-bin — the columnar Arrow / Parquet binding"},{"slug":"reference/cli","title":"CLI Reference","summary":"Every subcommand and flag the cx binary accepts.","text":"CLI Reference CLI Reference Every subcommand and flag the cx binary accepts. Format Conversion Input from a file or stdin; output to stdout. shell cx --cx pizza.cx # canonical CX form cx --xml pizza.cx # XML cx --json pizza.cx # semantic JSON cx --ast pizza.cx # AST as JSON cx --yaml pizza.cx | cx --toml # chain conversions From-To Flags shell cx --from=yaml --to=json < config.yaml cx convert config.yaml --to=json cx convert order.json --from=json --to=cx --compact Subcommands shell cx fmt FILE # lossless canonical formatter cx canonical FILE # strict canonical text cx hash FILE # SHA-256 over canonical bytes cx eq A.cx B.cx # exit 0 if data-equivalent cx diff A.cx B.cx # semantic diff cx lint FILE # style + correctness cx validate FILE --schema=S.cxs cx eval TEMPLATE.cxl --data=CTX.cx cx select EXPR --data=CTX.cx cx table info FILE cx scaffold KIND # drop a skeleton on stdout Include Resolution shell cx --xml --include-root=./conf main.cx # resolve [?cx include=…] cx eval template.cxl --include-root=./tpl # also activates [?include] Exit Codes 0 — success (no diagnostics at or above the configured fail-on threshold) 1 — diagnostics found (lint or validate) or comparison returned not-equal 2 — I/O failure, schema load failure, or unrecoverable parse error Related CXL language reference Language bindings"},{"slug":"reference/cxl","title":"CXL Language Reference","summary":"Every CXL directive and its slot shape.","text":"CXL Language Reference CXL Language Reference Every CXL directive and its slot shape. What CXL Is CXL is CX that does things. A CXL program is a CX document with directives — square-bracketed forms whose name starts with a question mark — that evaluate against an input tree and produce output. The same syntax is data, the same parser parses both. cx [h1 [?= //page/@title]] shell cx eval template.cxl --data=page.cx Substitution and Interpolation cx [?= EXPR] # substitute value at this position [?str-template 'Hi [?=name]!'] # text template with embedded substitution [?cx output-target=html] # configure the evaluator Control Flow cx [?if [cond, then, else]] [?if [[c1, v1], [c2, v2], [*, default]]] # multi-branch [?for x :in PATH :return BODY] [?for x :in PATH :where COND :return BODY] [?let x :be EXPR :return BODY] [?match [VALUE, p1, body1, p2, body2, [*, default]]] [?try [BODY, CATCH]] Filter Directives cx [?upper [STR]] [?lower [STR]] [?trim [STR]] [?length [SEQ]] [?count [SEQ]] [?sum [SEQ]] [?concat [A, B, …]] [?for-each [SEQ, fn]] [?filter [SEQ, pred]] [?fold-left [SEQ, init, fn]] [?fold-right [SEQ, init, fn]] Named Templates cx [?def name :params [a, b] :body [tag [?= a] [?= b]]] [?name 'one' 'two'] [?use name] # zero-parameter call sugar Function Values cx [?fn (x) { [?= x * 2] }] -> (x) { [?= x * 2] } [?partial [name, _, 2]] [?focus [?= _ + 1]] Includes cx [?cx include=partial.cx] # parse-time include directive [?include 'partial.cxl'] # eval-time include directive Related How CXL sees the data — context, bindings, scoping Includes — both forms"},{"slug":"reference/cxpath","title":"CXPath Functions","summary":"Reference for the XPath 3.1 / 4.0 function library available in CXPath expressions.","text":"CXPath Functions CXPath Functions Reference for the XPath 3.1 / 4.0 function library available in CXPath expressions. String Functions cx string-length(s) # codepoint count substring(s, start, len) contains(s, sub) # bool starts-with(s, prefix) ends-with(s, suffix) upper-case(s) lower-case(s) normalize-space(s) # collapse whitespace concat(a, b, …) string-join(seq, sep) replace(s, pat, rep) # PCRE matches(s, pat) # PCRE bool tokenize(s, sep) encode-for-uri(s) Node Functions cx name(n) # qualified name with prefix local-name(n) # name without prefix namespace-uri(n) position() # 1-based; current context last() # size of the current sequence count(seq) exists(seq) empty(seq) Numeric Functions cx number(x) # cast to numeric abs(x) floor(x) / ceiling(x) / round(x) sum(seq) / min(seq) / max(seq) / avg(seq) format-number(x, picture) Boolean and Logical cx true() / false() not(x) boolean(x) # effective boolean value Sequence cx head(seq) / tail(seq) reverse(seq) distinct-values(seq) remove(seq, position) insert-before(seq, position, items) subsequence(seq, start, len) Date and Time cx current-date() current-dateTime() current-time() year-from-date(d) / month-from-date(d) / day-from-date(d) seconds-from-dateTime(dt) / minutes-from-dateTime(dt) / hours-from-dateTime(dt) Higher-Order cx for-each(seq, fn) filter(seq, predicate) fold-left(seq, init, fn) fold-right(seq, init, fn) apply(fn, args) function-arity(fn) cx: Module cx cx:parse(text) cx:serialize(value) cx:canonical(value) cx:hash(value) cx:merge(a, b) cx:resolve-includes(value) log: Module cx log:info(msg, fields?) log:warn(msg, fields?) log:error(msg, fields?) log:debug(msg, fields?) log:trace(msg, fields?) log:with-context(fields, body) Related CXPath concept page CXL reference"},{"slug":"reference/data-bin","title":"data-bin: Arrow and Parquet","summary":"Bridges the CX Table API to Apache Arrow (in-process) and Parquet (on-disk).","text":"data-bin: Arrow and Parquet data-bin: Arrow and Parquet Bridges the CX Table API to Apache Arrow (in-process) and Parquet (on-disk). Purpose data-bin connects the CX Table API to the columnar formats the analytics ecosystem already speaks — Apache Arrow for in-process exchange, Parquet for on-disk storage. The bridges live in libcx_arrow; the CX side stays format-agnostic. Mapping A CX table converts to an Arrow record batch with one column per CX column, types preserved end-to-end. Decimal stays decimal, dates stay dates, sized integer widths preserved i32 stays i32 u16 stays u16, no ad-hoc string round-trips. The reverse direction reads Arrow or Parquet and projects into the CX Table form, where the rest of the Table API takes over. CLI shell cx table dump orders.cx --to=parquet > orders.parquet cx table load orders.parquet --to=cx cx table dump orders.cx --to=arrow > orders.arrow From Python python import cx, pyarrow as pa batch = cx.table_to_arrow(cx.parse(open('prices.cx').read())) print(batch.schema) Phase Status Phase A (Arrow round-trip) ships at v0.7.0. Phase B (Parquet writer) and Phase C (read-side schema inference) follow in v0.7.x. See spec/v0_7_0_status.md for current phase. Related Tables — the Table API source of truth ast-bin — the structural binary baseline"},{"slug":"reference/errors","title":"Error Codes","summary":"Catalogue of cx-err codes raised by the parser, the include resolver, and the evaluator.","text":"Error Codes Error Codes Catalogue of cx-err codes raised by the parser, the include resolver, and the evaluator. Wire Format Every CX error carries a structured payload: cx-err:CODE then a unit separator US 31 then a human-readable description then optionally another unit separator and a value. Bindings preserve this shape so ?try / ?catch can match on code rather than on text. CXER001x — Security Caps CXER0010 — function call depth exceeded CXER0011 — collection size cap reached CXER0014 — eval-time include attempted without --include-root CXER002x — cx: Module Must CXER0020 — cx:parse received a value that is not a string CXER0022 — cx:patch element-removed needs non-empty path CXER0024 — cx:merge encountered incompatible nodes CXER004x — Evaluator Gates CXER0040 — purity gate: SideEffect / ReadOnly filter raised under [?cx pure-only] CXER0041 — module gate: on_declaration module called without use-module CXER0042 — eval gate: cx:eval invoked without [?cx allow-eval] CXER0043 — eval depth gate: cx:eval recursion exceeded max-eval-depth E9xx — Include Resolver E901 — absolute path rejected E902 — path escapes include root (lexical or symlink) E903 — URL-scheme path rejected E904 — cycle detected E905 — max depth exceeded E906 — file not found E907 — file not readable E908 — path is a directory E909 — I/O error during read E910 — non-UTF-8 content E911 — included file parse failure Catching With try cx [?try [BODY, [?catch [err] [?if [$err:code = 'E906', file missing, other error: [?= $err:code]]]]]] Related CXL ?try / ?catch Includes — full E9xx semantics"},{"slug":"reference/grammar","title":"Grammar (EBNF)","summary":"Normative CX grammar in EBNF. The source of truth is spec/grammar.ebnf in the repo.","text":"Grammar (EBNF) Grammar (EBNF) Normative CX grammar in EBNF. The source of truth is spec/grammar.ebnf in the repo. Source of Truth The normative CX grammar lives at spec/grammar.ebnf in the repository. This page mirrors the high-level shape and points at the file for the full production list. The grammar is append-only after v0.7.0; new constructs add new productions and feature-flag through capability bits. Document ebnf Document ::= Prolog (Element | TopText | \"---\" Document)* Prolog ::= (XmlDecl | CxDirective | Comment | WS)* XmlDecl ::= \"[?xml\" Attr* \"]\" CxDirective ::= \"[?cx\" Attr+ \"]\" TopText ::= /* logfmt-mode top-level bare attributes */ Attr (WS Attr)* Element ebnf Element ::= \"[\" Name TypeAnno? Sigil* Attr* (WS Body)? \"]\" Sigil ::= \"&\" Name | \"*\" Name | \"#\" Name TypeAnno ::= \":\" TypeRef Attr ::= AttrSigil Name (\"=\" Value)? AttrSigil ::= \"+\" | \"-\" | ε Value ::= ScalarLit | Quoted | TripleQuoted | CollectionLit Body ebnf Body ::= BodyItem (WS BodyItem)* BodyItem ::= Element | QuotedString | TripleQuoted | BlockContent | RawText | LineComment | BlockComment | CollectionLit | TextRun TextRun ::= /* run of bytes excluding [, ], \\', \\\" until next boundary */ BlockContent::= \"[|\" /* parsed body, newlines preserved */ \"]\" RawText ::= \"[#\" /* nothing parsed, terminator is #] */ \"#]\" LineComment ::= \"#\" /* to end of line, outside an element body */ BlockComment::= \"[-\" /* … */ \"]\" Collection Literals (ADR 0017) ebnf SequenceLit ::= \"(\" (Value (\",\" Value)*)? \")\" ArrayLit ::= \"[\" (Value (\",\" Value)*)? \"]\" MapLit ::= \"{\" (MapEntry (\",\" MapEntry)*)? \"}\" MapEntry ::= MapKey \":\" Value MapKey ::= Name | Quoted CXL Directives ebnf Directive ::= Interpolation | EvalDirective Interpolation ::= \"[?=\" Expr \"]\" EvalDirective ::= \"[?\" Name (Slot | LabeledSlot)* \"]\" Slot ::= Expr LabeledSlot ::= \":\" Name Expr Type References ebnf TypeRef ::= IntType | FloatType | \"decimal\" | \"bigint\" | \"string\" | \"bool\" | \"null\" | \"date\" | \"datetime\" | \"bytes\" | TypeRef \"[]\" /* array of */ IntType ::= \"int\" | \"i8\" | \"i16\" | \"i32\" | \"i64\" | \"u8\" | \"u16\" | \"u32\" | \"u64\" FloatType ::= \"float\" | \"f32\" | \"f64\" Related Data model — semantics on top of the grammar Type system overview Error codes thrown by the parser and resolver"},{"slug":"reference/includes","title":"Includes","summary":"Two include forms, one resolver — parse-time for static composition, eval-time for dynamic.","text":"Includes Includes Two include forms, one resolver — parse-time for static composition, eval-time for dynamic. Two Forms, One Resolver CX has two include directives that share one resolver. The parse-time form expands files before the document reaches downstream tools; the eval-time form expands files while a CXL template runs. Both are opt-in via --include-root and both enforce the same containment rules. Parse-Time Include The parse-time form is the directive . The path resolves against --include-root; the directive is replaced in-place by the included document. Use this for static composition — config files, schema fragments, page templates whose paths are known up-front. shell cx --cx --include-root=./conf main.cx cx [users [?cx include=lib/admins.cx] [user name=carol]] Eval-Time Include The eval-time form is the directive . The path is any CXL expression — literal, bound variable, or computed via filters. The included file parses with includes resolved inside it, and evaluates inline at the directive site. cx [?include Both forms reject absolute paths (E901), paths that escape the root after lexical normalisation (E902), and URL-shaped paths (E903). Cycle detection (E904) and depth limits 0.0 default 8 protect against runaway recursion. See spec/include.md for the full error catalog. Related CXL language reference --include-root flag"},{"slug":"reference/schemas","title":"Schemas and Validation","summary":"Declare the expected shape of a document; validate before processing.","text":"Schemas and Validation Schemas and Validation Declare the expected shape of a document; validate before processing. What a CX Schema Is A schema in CX is itself a CX document. It declares which elements may appear where, what attributes they carry, and what types those attributes hold. The cx validate subcommand walks the schema against an input document and reports diagnostics. Minimal Schema cx [schema [element name='pizza' [attribute name='size' type='enum' values='small medium large'] [attribute name='crust' type='string']]] Validating a Document shell cx validate order.cx --schema=order.cxs cx validate order.cx --schema=order.cxs --mode=strict cx validate order.cx --schema=order.cxs --fail-on=warn Exit codes: 0 if no diagnostics at or above fail-on, 1 if any, 2 on I/O or schema-load failure. Validation Modes Three modes determine what counts as a diagnostic. Open admits undeclared attributes and child elements as warnings; strict rejects them as errors; closed treats undeclared content as a hard error. The schema may set its own default mode; the --mode flag overrides it. Type System Schemas declare attribute types from a fixed catalog — string, int with sized variants such as i8 / u16 / i64, float, decimal, bigint, bool, date, datetime, bytes, enum with explicit values, regex with a pattern. The validator coerces and bounds-checks each value against the declared type. Related cx validate flag reference Type fidelity vs JSON / YAML / TOML / XML"},{"slug":"reference/streaming","title":"Streaming","summary":"Event-driven parse and emit. Bounded RSS regardless of input size.","text":"Streaming Streaming Event-driven parse and emit. Bounded RSS regardless of input size. When to Stream Streaming parse and emit move bytes through the runtime without ever holding the whole tree in memory. Use streaming when the input is large enough that resident memory matters — log files, telemetry dumps, large XML / JSON / CXDB sources. The tree API materialises everything; streaming materialises nothing. Event Shape A streaming parse fires four event kinds: element_start (name plus attributes), attribute after element_start before any body, body chunk (text or scalar value), and element_end. The same event shape works on every input format — XML and JSON sources produce the same events. Streaming Parse python import cx with open('events.cx', 'rb') as f: for event in cx.stream(f): if event.kind == 'element_start' and event.name == 'error': print(event.attrs) Streaming Emit python with cx.stream_writer(open('out.cx', 'wb')) as w: for record in fetch_records(): w.write(record) RSS stays bounded regardless of the output size. Tier-1 bindings V Python Go ship the streaming writer at v0.7.0; Tier-2/3 follow in the v0.7.x window. Throughput The streaming evaluator runs at 340 MB/s on the comparable bench corpus M2 Pro libcx -prod — about 17 percent under the comparable JSON benchmark on the same workload. The bench harness lives in vcx/tests/runners/streaming_bench.v; run make bench to refresh. Backpressure The streaming sink uses a flush_after_bytes threshold to bound buffer residency. Default 64 KiB; lower for memory-constrained environments, higher to amortise callback overhead on small-emit workloads. Related Tables — the columnar form that streams natively data-bin — Arrow and Parquet bridges"},{"slug":"reference/tables","title":"Tables, Streaming, and Binary Formats","summary":"Tabular data, event-driven IO, and the binary forms underneath.","text":"Tables, Streaming, and Binary Formats Tables, Streaming, and Binary Formats Tabular data, event-driven IO, and the binary forms underneath. The Table API A CX table is a structural form for columnar data. Columns declare a name and a type; rows are records. The Table API exposes count, schema introspection, dump, and load — symmetric inverses with declared types preserved across formats. cx [:table [:col name='size' type='string'] [:col name='count' type='int'] [:row [size 'large'] [count 12]] [:row [size 'small'] [count 8]]] shell cx table info orders.cx # column / row counts, types, byte size cx table dump orders.cx --to=cx cx table load orders.csv --to=cx Streaming Parse and Emit Streaming parse and emit move bytes through the runtime without ever holding the whole tree in memory. Callbacks fire per element-start, attribute, body chunk, and element-end. The same callback shape works on every input format. python with open('events.cx', 'rb') as f: for event in cx.stream(f): if event.kind == 'element_start' and event.name == 'error': print(event.attrs) Use streaming when the input is large enough that resident memory matters — log files, telemetry dumps, large XML / JSON / CXDB sources. The tree API materialises everything; streaming materialises nothing. Streaming Emit python with cx.stream_writer(open('out.cx', 'wb')) as w: for record in fetch_records(): w.write(record) RSS stays bounded regardless of the output size. Tier-1 bindings V Python Go ship the streaming writer; Tier-2/3 follow in the v0.7.x window. ast-bin: Binary AST ast-bin is a binary serialization of the CXDM tree designed for fast cross-binding handoff and stable disk storage. Compact, versioned, and round-trip-byte-identical with the text form. Used for the FFI boundary in every binding and for canonical-bytes comparisons in the conformance suite. Capability bit 29 advertises v6; bindings query cx_features before reading a payload. data-bin: Arrow and Parquet data-bin connects the CX Table API to the columnar formats the analytics ecosystem already speaks — Apache Arrow for in-process exchange, Parquet for on-disk storage. The bridges live in libcx_arrow; the CX side stays format-agnostic. Phase A (Arrow round-trip) ships in v0.7.0; Phase B (Parquet writer) and Phase C (read-side schema inference) follow. shell cx table dump orders.cx --to=parquet > orders.parquet cx table load orders.parquet --to=cx Related Identity & canonical form Language bindings"},{"slug":"release-notes","title":"Release Notes","summary":"What shipped in v0.7.0 and v0.6.0, and how to migrate.","text":"Release Notes Release Notes What shipped in v0.7.0 and v0.6.0, and how to migrate. v0.7.0 — Format-Stability Boundary v0.7.0 is the API and format-stability boundary through 1.0. C ABI symbols are append-only from here; ast-bin format versions are forward-compatible; the cross-binding conformance suite gates every release. The release ships the v0.7.0 evaluator, the parse-time and eval-time include resolvers, the Table API, and the documentation generator. v0.7.0: Added CXL v0.7.0 — FLWOR for, multi-branch if, match, typeswitch, named templates, function values, partial application, error handling Include resolver — parse-time and eval-time, shared containment + cycle detection Table API with Tier-1 columnar surface Doc generator (this site) and cx lsp subcommand Streaming evaluator at 340 MB/s on the bench corpus — 17 percent under the comparable JSON benchmark v0.7.0: Changed ADR 0017 collection literals — sequence, array, map Bare prose with commas, em-dash, and equals parses as one Element body Substituted attribute values in body position auto-quote when they would re-tokenize as structure cx eval and cx --cx accept --include-root v0.7.0: Removed The W012 cx_eval_streaming stub is retired. Pre-G1 symbol names retired in favour of the renamed surface. See the migration section below. v0.6.0 v0.6.0 introduced the collection-literal trio sequence array map per ADR 0017, the cross-binding API contract, the V-native data binding lane, and the schema validator at full Tier-1 coverage. The release also closed the bounded audit and brought every Tier-1 binding to parity on the C ABI surface. v0.6 → v0.7 Migration Six breaking changes ship with v0.7.0. Each is mechanical to update. M1: C ABI Symbol Rename Symbols matching cx_eval_cxl_* were renamed to cx_eval_* at G1. Bindings using the old names will fail to link. Replace cx_eval_cxl with cx_eval; cx_eval_cxl_with_target with cx_eval — the target is now the third positional argument. M2: Attribute Rename The columns attribute on :table renamed to cols. Existing tables that used columns parse as undeclared and warn under strict mode. The cx upgrade-config subcommand rewrites them automatically. shell cx upgrade-config orders.cx > orders.v07.cx M3: File Renames ADR 0021 renamed CXDB to cx:database. Library file lang/*/cxdb.* files renamed accordingly; build configs that name the file explicitly need an update. M4: Binding Cut Per the binding tiering decision, Tier-3 bindings Kotlin Swift Ruby TypeScript V-cffi are no longer feature-gating for v0.7.0. They lag by one minor release. Production users on a Tier-3 binding should expect a smaller feature surface until the per-binding catchup ships in v0.7.x. M5: W012 Stub Removed The cx_eval_streaming stub from v0.6.0 is removed. Use cx_eval with the streaming variant of the entry point. The C ABI symbol cx_eval_streaming returns NotImplemented if linked against; bindings that probed via cx_features bit 27 should detect support before calling. M6: ADR 0021 Rename CXDB is now cx:database internally. The user-facing CLI surface is unchanged (cx table is still cx table); internal references in ADRs and spec docs updated accordingly. Related Comparison Performance FAQ"},{"slug":"showcase","title":"Showcase","summary":"Small examples that prove range without padding the spec.","text":"Showcase Showcase Small examples that prove range without padding the spec. This Site Is Built With CX The site you are reading right now is generated by a CX pipeline from CX sources. Every page lives at docs-src/content/<path>.cx as a CX document with a fixed schema; one CXL renderer (scripts/gen_docs/build.cxl) walks the schema and emits the body shape; a shell scaffold pipes the render-tree through cx --md (for the markdown target served on GitHub) and cx --xml (for the HTML target served here). Sidebar nav, code highlighting, the markdown-table to HTML-table transform, the include-style page composition — all of it runs on top of cx eval. No third-party static-site generator; the doc-gen is part of CX dogfooding itself. The pipeline shape, in one block. shell docs-src/site.cx # nav declaration docs-src/content/**/*.cx # one .cx page per route scripts/gen_docs/build.cxl # CXL renderer scripts/gen_docs/scaffold.sh # orchestration (~300 lines bash) scripts/gen_docs/style.css # dark code, sidebar, layout scripts/gen_docs/playground/ # in-browser sandbox assets scripts/gen_docs/highlight/ # regex-based syntax highlighter # pipeline per page: cx eval build.cxl --data=ctx.cx | cx --md | post_md > _docs_staging/<page>.md cx eval build.cxl --data=ctx.cx | cx --xml | post_html > _site_staging/<page>.html The page schema is a CX document the renderer reads, not a template language we ship alongside. Every page declares one element with title layout audience summary, one or more blocks, and a region that survives regeneration. Body items are , , , , , or the marker that becomes this sandbox on the playground page. cx [page id=overview [meta title=Overview layout=index nav-order=0 audience=evaluator summary='One bracket for every data format.'] [section id=hello title='Hello, CX' [prose CX is one syntax for both structured data and prose ...] [code lang=cx src=...]] [section id=next title='Where to Next' [list [item path=quickstart label='Quickstart']]]] The renderer (a CXL program) walks //page/section and dispatches per body element name via the XPath self:: axis. Section heading prefers @title over @id; the example-ref resolver looks up //example by id inline; the markdown-table pattern becomes a real HTML <table> in post-process. Nothing in the pipeline is opaque or third-party. CXL reference — every directive the renderer uses CXPath — the queries inside build.cxl Includes — the same pattern build.cxl uses for partials Evaluation model — the directive dispatch underneath Read the source: site.cx, build.cxl, scaffold.sh, and any of the docs-src/content/*.cx files in the repo. The doc-gen decisions log at docs-src/internal/DECISIONS.md captures every design choice — schema, sidebar, root-relative versus depth-relative hrefs, parser hazards to work around, link discipline. Logfmt Parser in Eight Lines of CXL CX as a log-line format. Take a logfmt-style line of key=value records and split it into structured CX. shell ts=2026-05-20T18:42:00Z level=info request_id=abc123 msg=ok cx [?for line :in tokenize(input, '\\n') :return [event [?for kv :in tokenize(line, ' ') :return [?let pair :be tokenize(kv, '=') :return [pair name=[?= pair[1]] value=[?= pair[2]]]]]]] cx [event [pair name=ts value=2026-05-20T18:42:00Z] [pair name=level value=info] [pair name=request_id value=abc123] [pair name=msg value=ok]] Build System in CX Targets, depends, run — declared in CX, evaluated by a thirty-line CXL driver. cx [targets [target name=all depends='lib bin'] [target name=lib depends='src/foo.o src/bar.o' run='ar rcs lib.a src/foo.o src/bar.o'] [target name=bin depends='main.o lib' run='cc -o bin main.o lib.a']] cx [?def build :params [name] :body [?for t :in //target[@name = $name] :return [?for d :in tokenize(t/@depends, ' ') :return [?build d]] [?run t/@run]]] [?build 'all'] Config Template With Inline Substitutions Config and interpolation in one file — same dialect everywhere. cx [server host=[?= //env/@host] port=[?= //env/@port] workers=[?if [//"},{"slug":"tooling/editors","title":"Editors and LSP","summary":"Syntax highlighting, completions, diagnostics. One installer script per editor.","text":"Editors and LSP Editors and LSP Syntax highlighting, completions, diagnostics. One installer script per editor. The CX Language Server A single `cx lsp` subcommand speaks LSP over stdio. Any editor that talks LSP can drive completion, hover, go-to-definition, diagnostics, formatting, and rename refactors against CX, CXL, and CXS sources. shell cx lsp # speaks LSP on stdin/stdout The server is built into the cx binary itself — no separate install. Editors point at the cx executable and the server starts on demand. Editor Installers The tooling/install/ directory in the repo ships one shell script per editor. Each script writes the editor config, registers the cx LSP, and drops the syntax-highlighting plugin. shell ./tooling/install/install.sh # interactive — detects editors on PATH ./tooling/install/install-vscode.sh # VS Code only ./tooling/install/install-nvim.sh # Neovim only ./tooling/install/install-lsp.sh # LSP wiring only, no extension ./tooling/install/uninstall.sh # remove everything VS Code The VS Code extension lives at tooling/vscode. Syntax highlighting is via a tree-sitter grammar (tooling/tree-sitter-cx); LSP integration uses the standard vscode-languageclient harness. Syntax highlighting for .cx / .cxl / .cxs files Diagnostics from cx lint and cx validate Hover documentation on directives and CXPath functions Format-on-save via cx fmt Neovim Neovim configuration goes under tooling/neovim. The install script writes the LSP client config into the user runtime path and adds the tree-sitter grammar. shell ./tooling/install/install-nvim.sh Other Editors Any editor with LSP support can drive cx lsp directly. Point the editor at the cx binary and register .cx, .cxl, and .cxs as the file types. Helix, Emacs (via lsp-mode), Sublime Text (via LSP plugin), and IntelliJ IDEA (via LSP4IJ) all work without further configuration. Tree-Sitter Grammar The tooling/tree-sitter-cx grammar is the source of syntax highlighting in every editor. Build with `tree-sitter generate` from the tooling/tree-sitter-cx directory; the prebuilt artifacts ship in the per-editor extensions. Related Install CX itself cx lint and cx fmt — the diagnostic backends"},{"slug":"tooling/fuzzing","title":"Fuzzing and Tests","summary":"Conformance, fuzz, bench. The discipline that keeps every release stable.","text":"Fuzzing and Tests Fuzzing and Tests Conformance, fuzz, bench. The discipline that keeps every release stable. Conformance Suite The conformance suite at conformance/*.txt is the cross-binding contract. Each fixture declares an input and an expected output out_cx out_xml out_json out_text or out_err. Every binding implements a runner that walks the fixture set. A release is gated on byte-identical output across every binding. shell make test # all bindings, all suites make test-vcx # V core conformance make test-rust # Rust binding make test-python # Python binding # … one target per binding Fuzzing The fuzzers live at vcx/tests/fuzz. They drive the parser, the evaluator, the include resolver, and the schema validator with structured-random input. Coverage-guided where possible; corpus-replay always. shell make fuzz # 60-second run of every fuzzer make fuzz-parser # parser only, runs until interrupted make fuzz-replay # replay the saved crash corpus Benchmarks Microbenchmarks at vcx/tests/runners/streaming_bench.v and eval_features_bench.v. Results land in target/bench.json; the cross-binding parity script scripts/run_bench_json.py compares per-binding times against the V baseline. shell make bench # runs the full bench harness scripts/run_bench_json.py --include-eval Related Performance — numbers and methodology cx lint, cx fmt, cx validate — production tools"},{"slug":"tooling/tree-sitter","title":"Tree-Sitter Grammar","summary":"Incremental parser for editors and static-analysis tools. Lives at tooling/tree-sitter-cx in the repo.","text":"Tree-Sitter Grammar Tree-Sitter Grammar Incremental parser for editors and static-analysis tools. Lives at tooling/tree-sitter-cx in the repo. Purpose Tree-sitter is the engine behind syntax highlighting, code folding, structural selection, and lightweight static analysis in modern editors. The CX grammar at tooling/tree-sitter-cx covers all of v0.7.0 and ships prebuilt artifacts inside each editor extension. Building the Grammar shell cd tooling/tree-sitter-cx tree-sitter generate tree-sitter parse example.cx Editor Bindings VS Code — packages the grammar as a .wasm artifact Neovim — picks up the grammar via nvim-treesitter Helix and Emacs (tree-sitter-langs) wire it through directly Queries Highlight, indent, fold, and locals queries ship alongside the grammar. They drive syntax colours, structural selection, and the cursor-jump-to-definition behaviour. The queries are versioned with the grammar; updates flow through the editor extensions on each release. Related Editor and LSP integration Grammar (EBNF)"},{"slug":"tour/data","title":"50 Ways to Love Your CX Data","summary":"Fifty short CX data examples, simple to complex. Pizzeria edition.","text":"50 Ways to Love Your CX Data 50 Ways to Love Your CX Data Fifty short CX data examples, simple to complex. Pizzeria edition. Read This First Fifty examples that walk through CX data — bare attributes through anchors and merge, with stops at quoted strings, multi-line text, comments, collection literals, typed columns, and a final whole-shop document. Each step adds one shape; no shape requires anything the earlier ones do not already give you. The companion 50 Ways to Love Your CXL Code covers CXL directives. 1. The Atom Just name equals value. In CX this is a valid document on its own — logfmt mode, intended for log lines and structured events. cx size=large 2. Several Atoms — a One-Line Event A whole log line — no brackets, no root, just attributes. cx time=2026-05-09T12:00:00Z level=INFO svc=oven pizza=Margherita 3. Empty Element Brackets give a name. Empty, but a thing. cx [pizza] 4. Element With One Property The atom from example 1, now attached to a pizza. cx [pizza size=large] 5. Several Properties Keep adding. No commas required between attrs. cx [pizza size=large crust=thin price=12] 6. Bare Flag (No Equals-Value) Presence means yes, absence means no — same idea as --verbose or HTMLs required. Here, this pizza is controversial. cx [pizza name=Hawaiian controversial] 7. Explicit Boolean Values When you want both polarities, write the word. cx [pizza vegan=true gluten-free=false] 8. Boolean Sigil Shorthand Plus-name means name=true; minus-name means name=false. Compact form for many booleans. cx [pizza +vegan -nuts -gluten] 9. Numbers — Int, Decimal, Negative, Hex, Underscores All bare, all auto-typed. Underscores in numbers are readability sugar. cx [pizza price=12.50 discount=-2 ovens=4 mask=0xFF00FF batch=1_000_000] 10. Quoted — Single Quotes Reads: attribute name, value is the string BBQ Chicken. cx [pizza name='BBQ Chicken'] 11. Quoted — Double Quotes Pick the quote that does not appear in the value. cx [pizza name=\"Quattro Formaggi\"] 12. Mix When the Value Has an Apostrophe Double-quote around an apostrophe; no escaping needed. cx [pizza name=\"Tonys Special\"] 13. The Empty String Distinct from missing: the attribute is there, just empty. cx [pizza name=Mystery description=\"\"] 14. Escape Sequences Inside Quotes Same family as C and JSON: backslash-n, backslash-r, backslash-t, backslash-uXXXX, backslash-quote, backslash-backslash. cx [pizza alert='Hot!\\\\nHandle with care.\\\\tCafe e'] 15. Entity References (XML-Style) Ampersand-amp-semicolon, ampersand-lt-semicolon, ampersand-gt-semicolon work as in XML. Useful when a literal ampersand would confuse the parser. cx [pizza name='Cheese &amp; Pepper' note='use <tongs>'] 16. Null A real null value, distinct from empty string or the word null as a string. cx [pizza discount=null reviewed=null] 17. Date and Datetime Literals ISO 8601 shapes are auto-typed as date or datetime. cx [promo starts=2026-05-01 ends=2026-05-31 cutoff=2026-05-31T23:59:00Z] 18. Plain Text Body After the name, the rest of the bracket is text. cx [motto Hand-tossed since 1987] 19. Attributes Plus Text Body Reads: element pizza, attribute name=Margherita, then text body Classic since day one. cx [pizza name=Margherita Classic since day one.] 20. Multi-Line Text — Preserved as Written Whitespace and newlines inside a text body are kept literally. cx [description Stone-baked, hand-stretched, and ready in 90 seconds.] 21. Triple-Quoted String Three quotes open and close. Single and double quotes inside need no escaping. Common leading whitespace is stripped. cx [bio Born in Naples, 1955. Started New Haven in 1987. Still on the line every Friday.] 22. Block Content [| … ] Like a text body, but indentation and newlines are kept verbatim. Unlike triple-quoted, inner elements such as best are still real markup. cx [poem [| Roses are red, violets are blue, the [em best] pizza is waiting for you. ]] 23. Raw Text [# … #] The CDATA of CX. Brackets, quotes, less-than, greater-than — all literal characters. The term"},{"slug":"tour/programs","title":"50 Ways to Love Your CXL Code","summary":"Fifty CXL directives, simple to complex. Pizzeria edition.","text":"50 Ways to Love Your CXL Code 50 Ways to Love Your CXL Code Fifty CXL directives, simple to complex. Pizzeria edition. Read This First Fifty short CXL examples — interpolation, paths, filters, conditionals, iteration, functions, modules — that walk from the simplest substitution to a full pipeline with module activation, HTML-safe escaping, function definition, structured logging, semantic merge, and iteration. Each example pairs a tiny data input with a directive snippet. 1. A Literal Value evaluates and prints. Here, the number 42. cx [?=42] 2. A Literal String Same form; strings and numbers interpolate identically. cx [?='Hand-tossed since 1987'] 3. Read an Attribute — @name @name is CXPath for the attribute named name. Prints Margherita. cx [pizza name=Margherita] [?=@name] 4. Find Anywhere — // // matches a pizza element at any depth. cx [order [pizza name=Margherita]] [?=//pizza/@name] 5. Inside Body Text A directive sits inside text. The rest stays as written. cx [pizza name=Margherita] [receipt Thanks for ordering [?=@name]!] 6. Inside an Attribute Value Interpolation works in attributes and in text. One mechanism, two positions. cx [user id=42 name=Joe] [a href=/u/[?=@id]/profile View [?=@name]s profile] 7. Several on One Line Each is independent. cx [pizza name=Margherita price=12] [line [?=@name] — [?=@price]€] 8. Inside a Generated Element Per iteration, build a fully-formed … element. cx [catalog [c cid=42 name=Joe] [c cid=43 name=Ann]] [?for c :in //c :return [a href=/u/[?=c/@cid] [?=c/@name]]] 9. Child Step Child steps separated by /. Prints Margherita. cx [shop [menu [pizza name=Margherita]]] [?=//shop/menu/pizza/@name] 10. Parent Axis — parent::* Walks up one element. Prints New Haven. cx [shop name='New Haven' [pizza name=Margherita]] [?=//pizza/parent::*/@name] 11. Ancestor Axis All ancestors of c. Prints mid;top; cx [a tag=top [b tag=mid [c found]]] [?for x :in //c/ancestor::* :return [?=x/@tag];] 12. Following-Sibling Axis Walks siblings to the right of the matched node. cx [row [cell n=1] [cell n=2] [cell n=3]] [?for x :in //cell[@n=1]/following-sibling::* :return [?=x/@n];] 13. Predicate Filter — [@attr=value] Brackets inside CXPath are predicates, not new elements. cx [menu [pizza name=Margherita] [pizza name=Hawaiian]] [?=//pizza[@name='Hawaiian']/@name] 14. Upper-Case Wrap a value with to call a filter. cx [pizza name=margherita] [?=[?upper [@name]]] 15. Default — Fall Back When Missing Graceful fallback; no exception. cx [pizza name=Mystery] [?=[?default [@price, 0]]] 16. Composed Filters — Nested Inside-out: trim, then upper. cx [pizza name=' margherita '] [?=[?upper [[?trim [@name]]]]] 17. String Length Counts characters. Prints 10. cx [pizza name=Margherita] [?=[?string-length [@name]]] 18. Todays Date , , . cx [?=[?current-date]] 19. Pipeline |> — Left-to-Right Composition Same result as composed filters, read top-down instead of inside-out. cx [pizza name=' margherita '] [?=@name |> trim |> upper] 20. Arrow => — Method-Call Style XQuerys invocation syntax. Reads as name.lower(). cx [pizza name=MARGHERITA] [?=@name => lower()] 21. String Concatenation || Join strings with double-pipe, like SQL. cx [user first=Ada last=Lovelace] [?=@first || ' ' || @last] 22. Range — M to N Inclusive range. Prints 1;2;3;4; cx [?for n :in 1 to 4 :return [?=n];] 23. If — Positional Form Three slots: condition, then, else. cx [pizza stock=0] [?if [@stock > 0, in stock, out of stock]] 24. If — Labeled Form Same semantics, named slots — usually clearer. cx [pizza stock=5] [?if @stock > 0 :then in stock :else out of stock] 25. Skip the Else Branch False condition renders nothing. cx [pizza featured=true] [?if @featured :then ★ featured!] 26. Existence Check A node-set is truthy when non-empty. cx [pizza [tags vegan]] [?if [//tags, yes, no]] 27. Multi-Branch — No Else-If Ladder Each is a branch; * is the catch-all. cx [pizza stock=2] [?if [[@stock > 100, plenty], [@stock > 10, some], [@stock > 0, last few], [*, sold out]"},{"slug":"tutorial","title":"Tutorial","summary":"Walk CX from atoms to closures in one scrollable page with exercises.","text":"Tutorial Tutorial Walk CX from atoms to closures in one scrollable page with exercises. Read This First This tutorial walks CX from the smallest piece to the fullest expression in one scrollable page. Each chapter introduces one shape, shows it in context, and ends with two or three exercises you can run with cx --ast or cx --json. Pace yourself. Assumed background: familiarity with at least one of JSON, YAML, TOML, or XML. CX assumes you know what an object, array, scalar, and attribute are. Chapter 1. Why CX Looks the Way It Does Most projects need two kinds of file: typed configuration in nested structures, and documents with prose plus structure. JSON, YAML, and TOML are optimized for the first; Markdown, HTML, and XML for the second. Projects end up with multiple file formats and multiple parsers. CX is one syntax for both. The same parser handles a Kubernetes-style config and an article with mixed-content prose. Five design constraints picked the shape: one construct, no indentation semantics, optional quoting, types first-class, lossless conversion to existing formats. Chapter 2. Elements Every CX node is a bracket pair with a name and an optional body. Editors balance brackets natively; code folding works without parser hooks. The opening name appears once; nothing repeats at close. cx [p Hello, World] Exercises. Save the snippet as hello.cx and run cx --ast hello.cx. Add a child element and re-run. Then run cx --json on the same file. Chapter 3. Attributes Attributes are name=value pairs after the element name. Bare values auto-type: 8080 becomes int, 3.14 becomes float, true becomes bool, 2026-01-01 becomes date. Leading-zero tokens stay strings — 02134 stays the string 02134 — to avoid the YAML Norway problem. cx [server host=api.example.com port=8080 ssl=true] Exercises. Add three more attributes and check what type cx assigns each. Quote a value containing a space and inspect the AST. Chapter 4. Types CX auto-types most values; for the rest, a colon-type annotation pins the value or body type. Sized variants carry range constraints that validate at parse time. cx [port :u16 8080] Sized integer variants i8, i16, i32, i64, u8, u16, u32, u64; sized float f32, f64; arbitrary-precision decimal and bigint. Exercises. Try setting :u16 to 70000 and observe the error. Compare 12 vs 12 in cx --ast. Chapter 5. Element Bodies A body holds Text, Scalar, child Element, or collection literal in source order. Mixed content is first-class — text and structure interleave the way they do in XML. cx [para Try the [b Margherita] — our [em best seller], hand-tossed daily.] Triple-quoted bodies preserve multi-line content verbatim. See 50 Ways to Love Your CX Data example 21 for the triple-quoted body shape. Exercises. Write an article with h1, p, em, and a children; convert with cx --md. Inspect a triple-quoted body with cx --ast. Chapter 6. Comments Hash line comments live outside element bodies. Bracket-dash block comments live inside elements and can span lines. cx [shop port=8080 [menu [pizza name=Margherita]]] Add a hash line above the shop element; add a bracket-dash inline. Exercises. Check what cx fmt preserves and what cx canonical drops. Chapter 7. Sigils Five sigils give CX structural reach beyond name and attribute. The at-sigil prefixes attributes in CXPath and references in body position. The ampersand declares an anchor; the asterisk merges from an anchor; the hash declares an id; the colon pins a data type. cx [defaults &shared timeout=30 retries=3] cx [server *shared name=api] Exercises. Declare an anchor and merge from it in two servers. Add ids to three pizza elements; reference them from a separate order element. Chapter 8. Collection Literals Three first-class collection shapes. Sequence with parens flattens; array with square brackets preserves nesting; map with braces gives named key-to-value entries. cx [pizza toppings=(cheese, basil, oil)] cx [pizza prices={small: 9, medium: 12, large: 15}] Exercises. Build a c"}];
